WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:24.710
I do not remember my name. This is the first

00:24.760 --> 00:32.670
thing that I think when I wake and I look around nervously, confused by the dark and by the thick

00:32.720 --> 00:39.810
fog which surrounds me. I raise a hand to my face and feel a short nose and a

00:39.860 --> 00:47.610
small mouth. I try to remember my face. I try to remember the color of my hair or what my eyes look

00:47.660 --> 00:54.290
like. I try to remember anything. But I cannot. I have

00:54.340 --> 00:58.400
no idea who I am. I am on the ground,

00:58.940 --> 01:02.640
lying on cold grass which is wet from the fog.

01:03.840 --> 01:11.330
And I am alone. Why am I here? I ask myself. But I have no answer. I do not even

01:11.380 --> 01:18.240
know where this is because the fog is so thick that I can only see for a few meters in any direction.

01:18.950 --> 01:25.770
I try to stand but then I realize that my head hurts and that there's a sharp pain behind my

01:25.820 --> 01:32.800
right ear. I carefully lift my hand and touch the large lump which is there. It hurts to touch

01:33.310 --> 01:39.350
and I shout in the fog, the sound lost in the dark of the night. When I pull my fingers away

01:39.400 --> 01:47.130
they are wet and even with no light the blood is bright and easy to see. I begin to feel more

01:47.180 --> 01:51.710
than nervous now. I begin to feel scared. I imagine

01:51.760 --> 01:57.350
I can see shapes and figures in the fog and I want to run. I feel that I need

01:57.400 --> 02:00.200
to run, that there is somewhere I must be,

02:00.520 --> 02:07.030
somewhere I must remember. But before I move I need to know something. So I push myself up and

02:07.080 --> 02:10.560
sit on the ground. I look carefully at my clothes.

02:11.580 --> 02:15.240
But they mean nothing. The jeans are new.

02:16.080 --> 02:20.960
But now they're dirty. Muddy stains cover the legs from the wet grass.

02:21.960 --> 02:28.930
The t-shirt is not familiar either, just simple and black. So I empty my pockets and at first

02:28.980 --> 02:35.040
there is nothing helpful there, no wallet, no keys and no phone. But then I see the picture

02:35.660 --> 02:42.050
and I stop. It's a woman and even in the dark and the fog I recognize her.

02:42.100 --> 02:50.020
Her long blonde hair is beautiful and her kind friendly blue eyes are perfect.

02:50.800 --> 02:58.640
Yes, I know her. And the thought is so strong that I smile despite the pain in the back of my head.

02:59.440 --> 03:03.250
But what's her name? I pull the last item from

03:03.300 --> 03:12.030
my pocket. It's a red serviette with a single

03:12.080 --> 03:17.780
I say quietly into the fog and the sound of my voice seems strange and cold.

03:19.520 --> 03:27.290
Catherine, I am sure that I know her. But I still do not remember why. Is she my wife? My

03:27.340 --> 03:32.620
girlfriend? I think that she is. And I suddenly feel afraid for her.

03:34.000 --> 03:41.130
Catherine, I shout into the dark. But there is no reply. I'm about to shout again when suddenly

03:41.180 --> 03:45.040
I remember something. I remember Catherine's face.

03:45.800 --> 03:51.200
But she's not smiling like she is in the picture and her blue eyes look scared and desperate.

03:51.940 --> 03:56.850
I try to remember the image and I see that there's a piece of cloth in her mouth

03:56.900 --> 04:03.220
so that she cannot speak and that she's tied to a large grey stone by thick white ropes.

04:04.280 --> 04:09.960
No! I cry out and I push myself to my feet to spite the pain in my head.

04:10.620 --> 04:12.940
I do not know what to do for a moment.

04:13.500 --> 04:17.580
I only know that I must find her. That I must help her.

04:18.340 --> 04:22.100
I think about the lump on the back of my head and the fresh blood on my fingers.

04:22.760 --> 04:26.960
Is there somebody in the fog? Somebody who has Catherine.

04:28.040 --> 04:29.410
Someone who wants to hurt her.

04:29.460 --> 04:33.780
Who wants to kill her? I want to shout again.

04:34.540 --> 04:37.970
But then I do not. If the person who has

04:38.020 --> 04:43.210
Catherine hears me, she is in danger. And another image comes

04:43.260 --> 04:47.490
to me. It's the face of a man. An ordinary

04:47.540 --> 04:54.930
face with small dark eyes and dirty brown hair. There's nothing cruel about the face but I know

04:54.980 --> 04:59.050
instantly that this is the man that has Catherine and

04:59.100 --> 05:01.720
I hate that face with all of my heart.

05:02.680 --> 05:07.520
I begin to walk slowly at first, unsure of the direction that I must go.

05:08.120 --> 05:11.910
It seems like the ground is moving slowly up and

05:11.960 --> 05:19.830
I believe that this is right. The image I remember of Catherine tied to the grey stone is in less fog and I think that it must be on higher

05:19.880 --> 05:24.100
ground. I begin to walk faster but I'm soon running.

05:24.920 --> 05:31.090
The fog surrounds me and the dark is without end. I try not to think of anything but

05:31.140 --> 05:38.430
the direction I'm moving in but I'm starting to remember things now. I remember Catherine. She has a

05:38.480 --> 05:43.730
black uniform which she always wears at work in the restaurant and on the uniform is a

05:43.780 --> 05:51.010
badge with her name. Hello handsome. She always says to me. Another day at the office and I

05:51.060 --> 05:58.230
never say much but I do not have to. We understand each other without words and I remember that after I

05:58.280 --> 06:04.750
eat I wait for her in the car park until she finishes work and she's surprised to see me there but

06:04.800 --> 06:06.500
I suppose that's just who I am.

06:07.220 --> 06:12.430
A romantic. Suddenly there's a sound like a gunshot and

06:12.480 --> 06:17.870
I fall. For a moment I think that I'm dead and that Catherine is alone tied to the grey

06:17.920 --> 06:23.830
stone in the fog but then I see the light in the sky and I realize that the shot was

06:23.880 --> 06:31.090
a flare. I watch the light fall and illuminate the hillside. Is it the man with the dark eyes

06:31.140 --> 06:35.020
and dirty brown hair? Does he know I'm here? Good.

06:35.660 --> 06:41.620
If he looks for me he's not with Catherine I think and I get up again and run faster now.

06:42.080 --> 06:48.940
Do I hear voices in the fog behind me? I try to turn to look but the fog behind me is too thick

06:49.600 --> 06:55.700
and I only see the occasional light in the distance but in front of me the fog seems to be thinner

06:56.420 --> 07:04.210
and I begin to slow. I'm scared now because I know that I'm near and in the dark I begin to see

07:04.260 --> 07:11.610
large grey stones standing like giants on the top of the hill. In amazement I think that I

07:11.660 --> 07:18.950
recognize this circle of ancient stones. Stonehenge I say. Yes

07:19.000 --> 07:26.510
a voice replies and this is where it ends and a tall man steps from behind one of

07:26.560 --> 07:30.510
the silent giants. I expect him to have dark eyes

07:30.560 --> 07:38.090
and dirty hair but he does not. His hair is blonde, his eyes light and I think that this is

07:38.140 --> 07:45.850
not the man who has Catherine. Maybe he's his friend, his partner and I'm about to run when I see the

07:45.900 --> 07:51.250
small gun in his hand. I just want Catherine I

07:51.300 --> 07:58.790
say but I can see the hate in the man's eyes and I know that the only thing I can do now is run to the stone where I know she's tied

07:58.840 --> 08:06.090
and try to escape into the fog with her. Don't says the man as if he can see my intention in my

08:06.140 --> 08:13.850
eyes but I have to. At first I think that maybe I'm quick enough but then I hear the snap

08:13.900 --> 08:20.590
of the gun and feel the explosion in my back. For a moment more I run and I can see the shape of the stone

08:20.640 --> 08:27.430
where Catherine is tied and I fall to the floor in front of it. Catherine I shout but there is no

08:27.480 --> 08:34.850
reply. When I look to see why I see that she is not there the white ropes are still tied to

08:34.900 --> 08:39.230
the stone but she is not. Catherine I say again

08:39.280 --> 08:46.250
smiling because she's all that matters and I can rest now because I know that she is safe. You

08:46.300 --> 08:49.280
got him detective? A voice says behind me.

08:49.860 --> 08:56.630
Yeah that's him. The tall man says. He matches the girl's description perfectly. Dirty brown hair,

08:56.680 --> 09:01.720
small dark eyes and look the blood behind his ear is where she hit him before she escaped.

09:02.360 --> 09:09.870
Good for her. Yeah we think she's his third victim this year so why didn't he run? Why wait

09:09.920 --> 09:17.530
here for us? The tall man is silent for a moment. Who knows? I don't want to understand the mind of

09:17.580 --> 09:23.790
these psychopaths. I'm just happy. Catherine is okay. Catherine is

09:23.840 --> 09:26.860
okay. Catherine is okay.

09:28.600 --> 09:31.870
I do not understand anything else that they say but

09:31.920 --> 09:34.590
I do understand these three words and I

09:34.640 --> 09:44.180
smile again. Catherine is okay. Rob from the rich.

09:46.160 --> 09:51.830
The sun is high above Canary Wharf, high and hot

09:51.880 --> 09:58.090
and looking down on the crowds of people which wait on both sides of the empty road. We are

09:58.140 --> 10:01.400
standing there, the three of us in front of the bank.

10:02.300 --> 10:04.470
We are standing there in front of the glass doors

10:04.520 --> 10:14.110
and no one is looking at us. They are all

10:14.160 --> 10:20.910
that the race is starting soon. I look at our reflections. We all have the same white and orange

10:20.960 --> 10:26.870
uniforms, the same caps, the same silver sunglasses. I almost

10:26.920 --> 10:32.350
do not recognize the two men beside me and I think that from a distance people cannot see

10:32.400 --> 10:36.330
that I am a woman. Okay this is it. 10 minutes

10:36.380 --> 10:41.470
exactly. Remember your jobs and remember no real names. I'm

10:41.520 --> 10:42.720
Robin, your little John,

10:43.320 --> 10:45.530
one of the men says to the other. And your

10:45.580 --> 10:49.430
Marion, he says to me, the only woman and he gives me a

10:49.480 --> 10:56.200
quick smile. Little John looks up. The camera is still in position. You cannot see us enter or leave.

10:56.980 --> 11:00.160
I feel sweat running down my neck. Here he comes.

11:00.720 --> 11:08.650
Remember the music Marion? Robin says, and then the guard is at the door, a short fat man who looks at us, smiles and then opens it

11:08.700 --> 11:13.800
without a single question. Your quick, the air conditioning only broke this morning.

11:14.520 --> 11:19.350
We know, little John says. We broke it. And the

11:19.400 --> 11:22.740
guard's smile disappears, but it's too late.

11:23.620 --> 11:27.620
Robin pushes him back into the bank and John follows with the ladders on his shoulders.

11:28.240 --> 11:33.340
I can see Robin's gun appear in his hand and then I'm inside too and I pull the door shut.

11:34.420 --> 11:38.970
Music Marion? Robin says, there is no stopping now. So

11:39.020 --> 11:44.770
I pull the small stereo from one of the black bags on my shoulder and I press play. Beethoven's

11:44.820 --> 11:51.530
piano concerto number two fills the marble stairs. We run up the stairs together, Robin with his

11:51.580 --> 11:58.590
gun at the guard's back. Then little John stops to cover the first camera. He climbs up the ladder in a second and we run into the main

11:58.640 --> 12:05.630
room of the bank. It's a large room with oak tables, a dark marble counter and expensive leather

12:05.680 --> 12:09.660
sofers. Behind the counter there are two women. Out here,

12:09.900 --> 12:15.250
now, I shout at them. To my left I see the short fat man sit down on the floor with

12:15.300 --> 12:21.850
his hands on his head. Then Robin runs to the office and kicks open the door. The two women move but

12:21.900 --> 12:27.470
slowly. One of them looks down at the counter where I know there is a secret alarm. Not if

12:27.520 --> 12:35.470
you want to live. I shout, on the sofa. I pull two blindfolds from a bag and cover their eyes. Then I

12:35.520 --> 12:37.480
tie their hands and I look at my watch.

12:38.220 --> 12:42.760
Seven minutes. I shout to little John and he covers the last camera with plastic.

12:43.640 --> 12:50.710
I take a deep breath. The music is so loud and the bank so hot. Can we do this? Can

12:50.760 --> 12:52.180
we really do this?

12:53.060 --> 12:55.520
Then Robin leaves the office and he has the manager,

12:55.940 --> 12:59.300
Mr Charles M. Hastings. Hastings.

13:00.540 --> 13:04.080
I hate the name and I hate the man. Tall,

13:04.800 --> 13:11.430
arrogant, dressed in his expensive suit with a watch that costs more than most people make in a year. What

13:11.480 --> 13:14.300
is this? He says and he looks nervous

13:14.740 --> 13:20.540
but not nervous enough. What do you think? Robin asks and he tells him to sit at one of the tables.

13:21.440 --> 13:26.180
Hastings looks around. You can't be serious. A bank robbery.

13:27.220 --> 13:28.600
And for a moment he looks surprised.

13:29.800 --> 13:34.110
But then he laughs. You idiots. This is an investment

13:34.160 --> 13:41.550
bank. There's no money here. Everything is done by transfer. And he looks at us all like we're

13:41.600 --> 13:44.800
children with that arrogant expression on his face.

13:45.600 --> 13:50.660
Good Lord. You should leave now before the police get here and maybe you can still escape.

13:51.280 --> 13:58.240
He laughs again. But I doubt it. Little John puts a blindfold on the guard. Only Hastings can see now.

13:59.020 --> 14:04.030
But we stand behind him. And for a few seconds there is only the sound of Beethoven as the music

14:04.080 --> 14:10.670
begins to reach its moto allegro. That's okay. Robin says and he picks up one of the bags and

14:10.720 --> 14:13.480
puts it on the table. We brought our own money.

14:13.760 --> 14:18.750
£20,000 in small notes. Banks are difficult to rob

14:18.800 --> 14:23.840
but houses aren't. Even big houses with alarms and safes.

14:24.580 --> 14:28.420
Like yours. What? Hastings says.

14:29.240 --> 14:33.170
And then he looks at the bag. My house. My

14:33.220 --> 14:37.730
money. You robbed my house. I can see sweat on

14:37.780 --> 14:41.410
Hastings face now. But it's on mine too. I look at

14:41.460 --> 14:46.800
my watch. Four minutes. But what do you want? You have my money.

14:49.400 --> 14:54.070
Robin laughs. We don't want it. Not that. We're not

14:54.120 --> 14:57.490
common thieves. And he throws the bag to little

14:57.540 --> 15:04.150
John. Get it ready. He says. And little John takes the ladders back down the stairs to the glass

15:04.200 --> 15:10.120
doors. Then what? shouts Hastings. And this is my part.

15:11.260 --> 15:16.050
I take the laptop from the bag and put it in front of him and open it. Do you know

15:16.100 --> 15:20.570
what this is? He looks. It's a bank account. A transfer.

15:20.620 --> 15:25.730
So? He says arrogantly. So we need you to enter

15:25.780 --> 15:32.690
your details and then transfer £240,000 to this account. Do it. And then we go. And

15:32.740 --> 15:36.100
he looks more than nervous now. He looks scared.

15:36.960 --> 15:39.180
No, I can't. I don't have that sort of money.

15:39.340 --> 15:42.280
I can't. I put the newspaper down on the computer.

15:43.040 --> 15:46.940
Really? He looks at the newspaper for a moment that says nothing.

15:47.700 --> 15:53.450
One minute. Says little John. Read it. Robin shouts. Hastings

15:53.500 --> 16:00.160
is silent. But Robin points the gun at him. Read it. He shouts again. Okay. Okay.

16:00.500 --> 16:07.930
Bank manager receives £240,000 salary despite bank failure. He stops and tries to turn to look

16:07.980 --> 16:10.880
at us. But Robin pulls the trigger back on the gun.

16:11.420 --> 16:16.300
You don't understand. Hastings shouts. It's more complicated than that.

16:17.020 --> 16:22.110
We understand that hundreds of local businesses are in serious trouble because of you and this

16:22.160 --> 16:28.340
bank, I say. And you now have a decision to make because we have no time to enter the details or,

16:28.640 --> 16:36.230
I say, and I know that this is it. It has to be now. Beethoven fills the silence again.

16:36.280 --> 16:41.980
And I look at Robin and see that there's sweat on his face too. But Hastings does not move.

16:42.760 --> 16:49.740
Then goodbye, Mr Hastings. Robin says. And he moves the gun. But no, please look.

16:50.420 --> 16:54.160
And Hastings enters his details. I do not believe it.

16:54.840 --> 16:56.040
And I take the computer.

16:56.920 --> 17:04.680
Well, Robin says, it's done. It's gone. Direct. No one can trace it. Then the music stops.

17:05.260 --> 17:07.420
And I want to smile that there is no time.

17:07.860 --> 17:14.690
In the distance I hear the sound of a gun and the crowd outside cheers. Robin takes the last blindfold from the

17:14.740 --> 17:16.820
bag and puts it over Hastings' eyes.

17:17.620 --> 17:23.060
And what now? Hastings says. You think you can just walk out of here? Robin laughs.

17:23.980 --> 17:29.950
Something like that? He says quietly. The police are out there. They'll see you. They'll stop you.

17:30.000 --> 17:33.020
Robin then puts his mouth to Hastings' ear.

17:33.680 --> 17:36.680
Not if there's a big enough distraction, he says.

17:37.300 --> 17:40.500
Oh, and just to let you know, there are no bullets in any of our guns.

17:41.460 --> 17:47.620
Hastings starts to shout, but we do not listen. This is it. We have to go now.

17:48.420 --> 17:52.780
We take our uniforms off and put them in the bag. Then we're at the doors.

17:53.620 --> 17:57.460
Outside we hear the crowd cheer and we see the first runners pass by.

17:58.160 --> 18:02.740
Then there are more. Many more. Hundreds more. Thousands more.

18:03.320 --> 18:07.320
And now we start to smile because we know we're nearly out.

18:08.140 --> 18:12.300
Now says Robin. And little John presses the control on the air conditioning.

18:13.200 --> 18:16.140
For a moment there is nothing. Then the fans start.

18:17.200 --> 18:22.400
And then money fills the air outside. 20,000 pounds.

18:23.360 --> 18:30.180
It flies over the heads of the crowd into the runners and into the road. And then there is nothing but chaos.

18:31.440 --> 18:34.460
And no one sees us walk slowly from the bank.

18:35.380 --> 18:36.900
And no one remembers us later.

18:37.920 --> 18:44.500
And maybe you want to know what we plan to do with the money. Unfortunately, this I can never tell you.

18:46.640 --> 18:48.320
The lock and the monster.

18:50.400 --> 18:53.460
No darlings. She means nothing to me. Less than nothing.

18:53.800 --> 18:54.360
I hate her.

18:54.840 --> 19:02.420
It's only you, Emily. I promise. What? Devorce her. That's impossible. Why?

19:02.800 --> 19:05.660
Because of the money, the house, the cars? No, Emily,

19:05.800 --> 19:07.240
of course. You're more important than them.

19:08.060 --> 19:13.040
No, no, listen. Don't go. Listen to me. When I return to London, everything is going to be different.

19:13.980 --> 19:17.020
I have a plan, darling. Darling, I have a plan.

19:17.700 --> 19:18.140
Emily?

19:19.040 --> 19:25.560
But there is no answer. And Oliver Stern puts his mobile back into his coat pocket and looks around.

19:26.420 --> 19:30.440
There is no one near to him to hear. He is alone on the jetty.

19:30.980 --> 19:36.040
Apart from the boat attendant at his chair 20 meters away, his feet on a boat and a cigarette in his mouth.

19:36.800 --> 19:43.860
And he cannot hear from there. Oliver is sure. But just to be safe, he decides not to phone Emily again.

19:44.740 --> 19:46.860
Perhaps the less she knows the better.

19:48.280 --> 19:54.160
A plan. Yes. He has a plan. A good plan.

19:55.120 --> 19:56.040
And a simple plan.

19:56.960 --> 20:03.790
He looks out across the horrible grey lock. The sun is almost down behind the green snowy mountains

20:03.840 --> 20:11.430
now. And it's colder than before. What sort of person wants to have a holiday in this remote,

20:11.480 --> 20:17.990
ugly part of the world? Then he hears the sound of his wife's laughter from the terrace bar of the

20:18.040 --> 20:22.270
hotel. And he shakes his head. The sort of person

20:22.320 --> 20:26.010
like my monster of a wife. He tries to picture

20:26.060 --> 20:33.020
Emily. Her eyes are so loving, her laugh so light and pretty. But when his wife laughs,

20:33.520 --> 20:39.150
she sounds like a donkey. He looks at the lock again at the jetty and the small boats waiting

20:39.200 --> 20:45.140
there for the tourists in the hotel. And he asks himself once more if he can do it.

20:48.480 --> 20:53.590
She bays again. And he knows that he can. It's

20:53.640 --> 20:57.070
10 to 6 now. The boats stop going out at

20:57.120 --> 20:57.360
6.

20:58.080 --> 21:03.920
And the sun is almost gone. She needs to come now, he knows, but he cannot shout for her.

21:04.420 --> 21:09.640
No, it's her idea to go on a boat. Everyone in the bar knows she wants to go.

21:10.100 --> 21:16.930
I just need to wait for her, like a good husband, he thinks. The type of husband who is happy to go

21:16.980 --> 21:23.580
out on the huge, horrible lock in the dark with his monster of a wife. But can she still swim?

21:24.480 --> 21:30.030
He's not sure. He thinks that with her clothes and her coat on, she'll be too heavy and that the

21:30.080 --> 21:33.160
cold grey water of the lock will pull her down.

21:33.960 --> 21:34.840
But is that enough?

21:35.840 --> 21:42.960
He looks at the bag by his feet, the romantic picnic, filled with all the food his wife loves

21:43.740 --> 21:50.920
and the bottle of champagne that he knows she cannot resist. Oliver smiles. It is a heavy bag.

21:51.760 --> 21:57.960
All he has to do is make her hold it for a second, perhaps quickly wrap the straps around her hands.

21:58.680 --> 22:06.580
Then, no more monster. But it's nearly six now. The attendant is about to finish for the night.

22:07.160 --> 22:13.680
Excuse me? Oliver shouts with a smile. He pulls his cigarette packet from his pocket and walks over.

22:14.420 --> 22:16.180
Sorry to bother you, but do you have a lighter?

22:17.080 --> 22:20.900
The attendant has a hard face and fierce red hair.

22:21.520 --> 22:27.240
He looks at Oliver for a moment. Nods pulls his lighter from his pocket and offers it.

22:27.960 --> 22:32.500
Look, continues, Oliver. My wife wants to take a boat out. She's going to be here in a second.

22:33.320 --> 22:37.420
The man looks at the sky. A boat, man. We want a boat.

22:38.380 --> 22:45.840
Dinner, can I boot that? Better than Marla. The idiot does not speak English. All of the things.

22:46.740 --> 22:51.160
Bloody country. I'm sorry. I don't understand. I'm from England.

22:52.100 --> 22:59.660
The man laughs, stands up and says nothing. Oliver tries to stay calm, but he knows there's too little time.

23:00.440 --> 23:04.860
Don't you speak English? My wife wants to go out now. She's only going to be one minute.

23:05.440 --> 23:11.400
He shouts and the attendant stops and gives him a long, hard look with his cold blue eyes.

23:12.100 --> 23:16.650
Oliver, here I am. A loud, cheerful voice says. And

23:16.700 --> 23:20.220
the two men turn to see Mrs. Sylvia Stern.

23:20.960 --> 23:25.470
She smiles that stupid cow-like smile at them. And

23:25.520 --> 23:28.340
Oliver sees the idiot attendant smile back.

23:29.040 --> 23:35.310
She is dressed in a horrible tartan skirt and woolen jumper. And in her arms she has the heavy

23:35.360 --> 23:41.550
wax coat. Good. Oliver thinks. Darling, he says with a

23:41.600 --> 23:48.100
smile. This good man wants to close. I think we must wait until tomorrow now. Oh, no, surely not.

23:48.560 --> 23:50.440
She turns to the man and smiles again.

23:51.000 --> 23:52.940
Can't we just go out for a few minutes, please?

23:53.660 --> 23:54.760
Just to enjoy the sunset.

23:55.660 --> 23:58.860
And the attendant ignores Oliver, but returns her smile.

23:59.780 --> 24:06.350
Aye. Well, just for a 10 minutes then. Oh, thank you, Sylvia says. And the attendant helps them

24:06.400 --> 24:12.150
into the small rowing boat and passes Oliver the picnic bag. He then gives him another long,

24:12.200 --> 24:15.220
hard look. But Oliver does not care.

24:16.300 --> 24:22.220
Bloody idiot. He says to Sylvia as they slowly move away from the jetty onto the dark water.

24:22.960 --> 24:28.890
I don't understand a thing, he says. But Sylvia does not seem to hear. And instead looks out

24:28.940 --> 24:34.250
at the dark lock. For five minutes, Oliver rose. And

24:34.300 --> 24:36.990
soon the jetty and the hotel look much smaller.

24:37.040 --> 24:41.960
While the attendant is almost impossible to see in the half light of the new evening.

24:43.200 --> 24:47.610
And so this is it. Oliver thinks about loving ironic

24:47.660 --> 24:55.040
Emily again. Then he looks at his monster of a wife. Oh yes, this has to be it.

24:55.780 --> 24:59.190
No more listening to that awful laugh. No more looking

24:59.240 --> 25:05.700
at her boring face. And perhaps more importantly, no more sharing his bank account with her.

25:06.460 --> 25:11.480
Darling, he says, shall we have a little drink? Oh yes, that would be very nice.

25:12.040 --> 25:16.700
She smiles, but in the half light Oliver thinks he sees something strange in her expression.

25:17.500 --> 25:21.870
Can you pass me over the bag? Then you can come and sit here next to me. He says as casually

25:21.920 --> 25:29.490
as he can. Of course. Sylvia says and she stands up. Which makes the boat rock. Oliver thinks

25:29.540 --> 25:35.830
that this is going to be very easy indeed. Just one push. And then he can shout for the attendant

25:35.880 --> 25:42.430
and pretend to help but really just watch the monster go down into the lock. Just one look back

25:42.480 --> 25:44.660
at the jetty to be sure no one can see,

25:45.160 --> 25:49.120
he thinks. And he turns his head. And then it happens.

25:50.120 --> 25:56.390
Pain explodes at the back of his head and his eyes go dark. For a second he does not know where he

25:56.440 --> 26:02.600
is. Then he feels hands on him. Pushing him and moving him. As he tries to say something,

26:02.700 --> 26:08.910
he sees the broken champagne bottle at his feet. Then he falls from the boat. And the ice cold water

26:08.960 --> 26:15.290
takes the words and the air from his mouth. For a moment he goes down, but then he kicks his legs

26:15.340 --> 26:17.490
and he comes to the surface and looks up at

26:17.540 --> 26:23.700
Sylvia in the boat. She has an oar in her hands and she's reaching it out to him. Yes, closer, help me.

26:24.120 --> 26:26.690
He shouts and it's then that he sees the strange

26:26.740 --> 26:32.090
expression in her face again. Sylvia, darling, don't you dare

26:32.140 --> 26:39.310
call me that. She says in a tone as cold as the water. You're a cheating, lying, horrible

26:39.360 --> 26:46.010
monster of a man. You can call that stupid girl of yours, darling, but not me. Never again, Oliver

26:46.060 --> 26:52.770
Stern, never again. And then the oar hits him on the head and he cannot think and he cannot

26:52.820 --> 26:56.970
swim and the water takes him. For a few moments

26:57.020 --> 27:01.680
he sinks slowly into the great lock. But then somehow,

27:02.380 --> 27:09.390
miraculously, he kicks again and he comes to the surface once more. Sylvia and the boat are

27:09.440 --> 27:13.340
gone. And the half light is complete darkness now.

27:14.040 --> 27:17.570
He tries to swim, but his clothes and his coat

27:17.620 --> 27:24.070
are too heavy. The jetty, the attendant. He has to still be there. If he hears me, he can

27:24.120 --> 27:28.060
bring a boat out. Help me. He shouts into the darkness.

27:28.740 --> 27:35.460
Help me. For the love of God, help me. And on the small jetty on the bank of Loch Ness,

27:35.960 --> 27:39.600
the attendant hears. He takes the cigarette from his mouth and smiles.

27:40.480 --> 27:47.060
Sorry, ladie. He says quietly to the lock. I can't understand the word you're seeing.

27:49.700 --> 27:50.820
Follow me.

27:53.400 --> 28:01.250
Follow me. Says Detective Branwell with a sigh. His shift finishes in five minutes,

28:01.300 --> 28:04.460
but there's no one around in Howarth's quiet police station.

28:05.240 --> 28:12.000
And so he leads the woman to the first interview room and tries to suppress the image of a pint of beer in the black bull pub.

28:12.920 --> 28:16.420
The interview room is cold, the light's weak and gray.

28:17.380 --> 28:22.730
But he offers the woman a seat and hopes that this will be quick. As she takes her coat

28:22.780 --> 28:27.270
off, Branwell watches her. She has long brown hair

28:27.320 --> 28:34.250
that right now looks a little untidy. And she has deep green eyes that at the moment are tired and

28:34.300 --> 28:38.080
nervous and shows something which he does not completely recognize.

28:39.000 --> 28:44.260
Right, he says, and tries to smile to relax her.

28:45.160 --> 28:46.680
What's all this about then?

28:47.480 --> 28:50.360
The officer at the front desk says you need to speak to someone.

28:51.160 --> 28:55.870
I do, she says, and her voice is thin and

28:55.920 --> 29:00.890
tense. About what? She waits for a moment, uncertain,

29:00.940 --> 29:08.570
and then she takes a breath. About a crime. Branwell pulls his notepad from his pocket and places

29:08.620 --> 29:14.100
it in front of him on the table. He has a bad feeling about his pint in the black bull pub.

29:15.260 --> 29:21.660
I see, he says. Maybe you can tell me your name first. You're addressed too.

29:22.860 --> 29:27.940
She puts her hands on the table and he sees that her red fingernails are chipped and bitten.

29:29.120 --> 29:33.540
Jane Thornton, I live at brow top cottage. Brow top?

29:34.220 --> 29:41.530
Oh yes, I remember. You have that little red sports card, don't you? Not now, no. She says and

29:41.580 --> 29:46.010
she picks up her handbag. Can I smoke? No, sorry

29:46.060 --> 29:52.570
love, rules and regulations. I can get you a tea if you like. But she shakes her head and puts the

29:52.620 --> 29:59.840
bag at her feet. And maybe you can tell me why you're here, he suggests. Again, she waits.

30:00.920 --> 30:05.300
And again, Branwell thinks that he sees something in her deep green eyes.

30:06.320 --> 30:10.340
There's a man who follows me. She begins. Every evening.

30:10.980 --> 30:13.560
Every evening when I return home from work.

30:14.800 --> 30:22.650
Branwell nods. I see. No, you don't. Every evening. Every

30:22.700 --> 30:26.340
single evening. For almost a year now,

30:26.780 --> 30:31.930
he's always there detective. Sometimes he's so close that I think he can touch me and other

30:31.980 --> 30:35.630
times he's far away. But I still know that he's

30:35.680 --> 30:40.010
there. And detective, you might think that I'm crazy,

30:40.060 --> 30:45.920
but I know what this man wants. What? Branwell says.

30:46.640 --> 30:47.760
What does he want?

30:48.680 --> 30:53.720
She pauses for a second and he sees her hands shake. He wants to kill me.

30:54.560 --> 30:59.530
Branwell says nothing but watches the woman's face. She is

30:59.580 --> 31:03.330
scared. Very scared. Does he speak

31:03.380 --> 31:09.300
to you, this man? No, never. And when I try to speak to him, me...

31:09.840 --> 31:15.800
Yes? He disappears. Branwell opens his notebook and takes his pen out.

31:16.600 --> 31:22.210
Where do you work, Mrs. Thorne? Miss Thorne. I work

31:22.260 --> 31:25.440
in Leeds. I have a small media company.

31:26.380 --> 31:30.320
In Leeds. Then where does this man follow you from?

31:31.200 --> 31:34.380
From the station at Keithley. You work in Leeds,

31:34.660 --> 31:39.970
but you don't drive. I think your sports car can get you there a lot faster than the bus to

31:40.020 --> 31:43.530
Keithley and then the train to Leeds. I told you, I

31:43.580 --> 31:48.040
don't drive it. Not now. Right. So he followed you.

31:48.900 --> 31:52.200
From Keithley. I see him at the train station sometimes.

31:53.020 --> 31:57.600
Then he's there again when I get off the bus in the town centre here in Arworth.

31:58.320 --> 32:04.820
And then he follows me to my cottage. You walk from here to brow top, up over the moors road,

32:05.460 --> 32:11.280
every evening. That's a dangerous road. And it must be more than three miles.

32:12.540 --> 32:16.250
Dangerous, yes. But she does not say anything else and

32:16.300 --> 32:17.200
Branwell continues.

32:18.100 --> 32:19.780
Can you describe the man?

32:20.720 --> 32:27.860
Easily, she says. But again she pauses and Branwell sees her turn quickly to look over her shoulder.

32:28.740 --> 32:33.200
His tall with dark hair, his skin is pale and his eyes cold.

32:34.060 --> 32:41.690
Dead, she says. He has dead eyes. And they watch me every day and I think that they watch me

32:41.740 --> 32:42.540
at night too.

32:43.940 --> 32:49.980
Branwell feels a shiver run over his back. You think that this man enters your premises.

32:51.180 --> 32:54.900
That he enters your cottage during the night. I do.

32:55.760 --> 32:58.180
Sometimes I wake and I know that he's there.

32:58.900 --> 33:03.640
I turn on the lights and I see nothing but when I go to the windows I can see him in the field.

33:04.520 --> 33:10.460
He stands there like a scarecrow in the dark and he accuses me every day.

33:11.340 --> 33:16.060
His cold dead eyes accuse me. Branwell shakes his head.

33:16.780 --> 33:19.900
Is she crazy or just terrified?

33:21.280 --> 33:25.790
Miss Thorne, if this man enters your house we can

33:25.840 --> 33:29.500
arrest him. But to do that we need evidence.

33:30.660 --> 33:38.330
As he broke in a window or entered the property by force she lets out a laugh, a laugh that is

33:38.380 --> 33:44.850
horrible to Branwell. No. Of course not. There's nothing. And

33:44.900 --> 33:46.420
he never says anything threatening.

33:47.000 --> 33:54.690
He never says that he wants to hurt you. She looks over her shoulder again quickly and automatically as if she does it a hundred times a

33:54.740 --> 33:57.420
day. No. He never says a thing.

33:58.720 --> 34:05.880
But I know. I know what he wants. I know what he wants. And she begins to cry.

34:06.820 --> 34:12.920
Branwell puts the notepad away. I'm sorry Miss Thorne but there's very little that we can do.

34:13.660 --> 34:18.170
Let me make you that tea. A nice tea to calm you down. Let me can start again and see if there

34:18.220 --> 34:25.170
are any other details. She seems to nod and he stands up and goes to the door. We need a better

34:25.220 --> 34:27.800
description you see if we want to identify the man.

34:28.360 --> 34:31.360
What? She says before he can open the door.

34:32.040 --> 34:36.750
Oh no no. You don't understand the detective. I know who the man is. I know his name. I know

34:36.800 --> 34:44.480
everything. Branwell stops. You mean? She continues. And if I tell you his name you can help me.

34:45.020 --> 34:52.860
You can stop this. She says desperately. You know his name. Do you promise? You can stop it.

34:53.660 --> 35:00.190
Branwell does not sit. He sees that expression in her eyes again. I can't promise love. No.

35:00.240 --> 35:08.100
But I can try. She nods and bites her red fingernails for a moment. His name is William Gray.

35:09.200 --> 35:16.270
William Gray. Branwell knows the name. William Gray. And he

35:16.320 --> 35:21.590
remembers something horrible from more than a year ago. An accident on the roads

35:21.640 --> 35:26.350
across the moors. William Gray. A local cyclist

35:26.400 --> 35:32.010
and a good man. William Gray. Miss Thornton. William Gray

35:32.060 --> 35:38.690
is dead. He was it by a car last year while he cycled across brow top road. We never found

35:38.740 --> 35:41.520
the driver but William died instantly they say.

35:42.240 --> 35:49.070
Oh I know. She says and he sees that something in her deep green eyes. And he thinks that he knows

35:49.120 --> 35:56.770
what it is now. I know detective because I am that driver. Then there is only the sound of

35:56.820 --> 36:03.190
her crying to fill the cold interview room. And Branwell sits down again and picks up his pen and

36:03.240 --> 36:10.610
notepad. I think you should tell me everything. He says and he can see clearly now the guilt

36:10.660 --> 36:17.710
that fills her deep green eyes. An hour later Branwell closes the door of the interview room

36:17.760 --> 36:21.820
and sighs. He does not feel any sympathy for her.

36:22.260 --> 36:24.840
He cannot. Not for someone who can do that.

36:25.940 --> 36:33.130
To leave someone on the gray moors to die. No he cannot feel any sympathy for her. But maybe

36:33.180 --> 36:39.880
now she can find some rest. Maybe now she can forget the ghost of William Gray.

36:41.020 --> 36:46.230
He walks up the corridor to the front desk and is about to shout for an officer when he sees the

36:46.280 --> 36:49.200
tall figure standing at the door of the police station.

36:50.140 --> 36:53.820
He stops. His heart suddenly beating faster.

36:55.960 --> 36:59.000
The man's dark hair is wet from the rain outside.

37:00.340 --> 37:03.220
His skin pale and his eyes cold and dead.

37:04.100 --> 37:09.480
It's him. Branwell knows it. But he cannot believe it.

37:10.780 --> 37:18.320
Do you have her confession? The figure says. What? Do you have Miss Thornton's confession?

37:19.280 --> 37:24.950
The figure repeats. Branwell nods. Yes I do. She's in

37:25.000 --> 37:27.720
the interview room now. The figure nods.

37:28.480 --> 37:34.540
Good. Then it's over. And the impossible figure turns to leave.

37:36.080 --> 37:40.340
Branwell still cannot believe it. Yet the similarity is perfect.

37:41.340 --> 37:44.520
Wait Mr. Gray. William, wait. I need to ask you.

37:45.100 --> 37:52.570
But the figure turns and smiles. It's a cold smile but Branwell sees that the man's eyes look a little

37:52.620 --> 37:57.750
more alive now. My name is Gray Detective but not

37:57.800 --> 38:02.640
William. My name is Matthew. Matthew Gray.

38:03.280 --> 38:11.080
William was my brother. He says as he opens the door to the rain. My twin brother.

38:15.640 --> 38:22.150
How far is far enough? Jerry pushes open the door

38:22.200 --> 38:28.340
of the small Australian pub and steps onto the terrace with a pint of cold beer in his hand.

38:29.520 --> 38:36.130
Inside the pub it's small and dark and the barman is rude and suspicious but Jerry has to agree

38:36.180 --> 38:38.630
that the view from the terrace is more than

38:38.680 --> 38:45.050
spectacular. Red sandy plains stretch out in almost every direction

38:45.100 --> 38:55.750
and only to the east are their

38:55.800 --> 39:01.260
down at one of the small wooden tables and places his bag carefully next to his feet.

39:02.320 --> 39:04.770
The rest of his luggage is in the pickup truck

39:04.820 --> 39:10.840
but not this bag. No. This one stays with him.

39:12.060 --> 39:18.130
He takes a cigarette from his packet, lights it and lets the hot evening sun shine down on his

39:18.180 --> 39:24.270
bald head. The outback. This is it. This is far

39:24.320 --> 39:28.150
enough. And for the first time in a week Jerry

39:28.200 --> 39:34.050
relaxes and lets his anxious pale face smile. You do

39:34.100 --> 39:37.480
not steal from Big Jones and stay in London.

39:38.460 --> 39:46.140
Everybody knows that and Jerry understands better than most people exactly how true that is.

39:46.860 --> 39:50.330
No. You either get out of the city for life

39:50.380 --> 39:56.060
or Big Jones finds you. And when Big Jones finds you,

39:56.400 --> 40:04.300
that is when you go see the world. In little pieces. Most people think it's just a story

40:04.960 --> 40:09.810
but not Jerry. No. Big Jones does not just kill

40:09.860 --> 40:16.850
you. He takes you to one of his old factories on the Thames and then he shows you the boxes and

40:16.900 --> 40:24.630
the addresses of all the different locations in the world. And then he starts to chop. Jerry feels

40:24.680 --> 40:28.930
sweat run down his back. But he laughs and takes

40:28.980 --> 40:33.310
a long drink of the cold beer. Well, Big Jones

40:33.360 --> 40:37.520
cannot chop me up if he cannot find me, he thinks.

40:38.120 --> 40:43.200
And he looks at the bag at his feet. It was easy to get it through customs.

40:44.440 --> 40:51.460
They look for drugs and weapons. They do not look for 60,000 pounds of uncut diamonds.

40:52.620 --> 40:55.720
And he smiles and looks up at the hot sun.

40:56.760 --> 40:58.960
Well, someone looks bloody happy.

41:00.040 --> 41:06.520
A voice says and Jerry jumps. He looks forwards but cannot see because of the sunlight.

41:07.520 --> 41:12.140
Then two tall, strangely dressed men walk into the shade of the terrace.

41:12.940 --> 41:17.060
One with three dead rabbits in one hand and a long rifle in the other.

41:18.200 --> 41:24.030
G'day! says the other one. They both look similar. Messy

41:24.080 --> 41:31.210
blonde hair and a hat that are the same color as their khaki shorts and shirts. And they are

41:31.260 --> 41:37.260
both dirty. Their skin, boots and clothes all covered in red sand.

41:38.340 --> 41:40.420
Even him? Jerry says.

41:41.360 --> 41:45.780
Huh, a bloody palm. Says the first and drops the rabbits on the table.

41:46.540 --> 41:48.140
Is that right? Are you a palm mate?

41:48.780 --> 41:50.340
A what? asks Jerry.

41:51.140 --> 41:53.780
A palm. You know, an English bloke.

41:54.360 --> 41:55.760
Oh, yes.

41:56.800 --> 42:00.940
Nice one. Says the first before he disappears into the bar.

42:01.580 --> 42:06.260
Jerry is not sure but he thinks that the man looked at the bag by his feet.

42:07.060 --> 42:11.080
Are you on holiday, mate? asks the other who sits down at the table.

42:11.800 --> 42:13.720
Yeah. Something like that.

42:14.460 --> 42:17.980
Yeah? Where are you staying? There are no hotels around here.

42:18.960 --> 42:23.420
Jerry considers lying. But Big Jones can never find him here.

42:24.260 --> 42:29.870
Upper of Farmer Hobbes Creek. Oh, is that right? Me and Darwin live near there.

42:29.920 --> 42:34.460
It looks like we're neighbors. Darwin? Yeah, my bloody brother.

42:35.080 --> 42:37.840
I'm Charles. He's Darwin. Here he is with the stubby's.

42:38.520 --> 42:42.920
And the brother called Darwin comes out of the bar with two small bottles of beer.

42:43.880 --> 42:46.400
The palm is staying at the old Hobbes farm, Darwin.

42:47.280 --> 42:50.100
Well, you're no worse then. Be careful up there, mate.

42:50.320 --> 42:51.640
That's salty territory now.

42:52.520 --> 42:56.900
And both of the brothers smile. Salty territory? What's that?

42:57.800 --> 43:02.600
Crocs, mate. Crocodiles. There are a lot of them up there in the creek. Best be careful.

43:03.520 --> 43:05.740
And they laugh again. And Jerry smiles.

43:06.860 --> 43:12.980
Yeah, sure. I'll be careful. He says and he thinks he sees one of the brothers look at his bag again.

43:13.960 --> 43:17.380
Well, it's nice to meet you both but I need to get up to the farm before dark.

43:18.280 --> 43:20.960
Sure, mate. No worries. Says Charles.

43:22.000 --> 43:25.660
Watch out for the salties, mate. They can chop you up into little pieces.

43:26.490 --> 43:31.040
Says Darwin and Jerry thinks about big Jones and the old factory on the Thames.

43:31.980 --> 43:36.960
I'll be okay. See you later. He says to the brothers, then he heads for the truck.

43:38.120 --> 43:44.140
A bloody palm. Says Charlie quietly. He seems to like that bag very much.

43:44.780 --> 43:50.340
Says Darwin. He sure does. You know, I don't think he believes us about the salties.

43:51.060 --> 43:53.800
Bloody palms never believe us about the salties.

43:54.760 --> 43:59.830
And the brothers laugh as they watch Jerry and the truck drive up the road to Hobbes Creek

43:59.880 --> 44:04.340
while the great Australian sun begins to fall behind the horizon.

44:05.660 --> 44:12.220
It's almost dark when Jerry reaches the farm. The building is old but in okay shape.

44:13.340 --> 44:19.230
And he turns the generator on and the electric lights start. For half an hour he unpacks his

44:19.280 --> 44:24.900
things. Then he sits down on the terrace with his bag at his feet and lights another cigarette.

44:26.260 --> 44:32.610
The creek is 20 meters from the farmhouse and the evening seems to be alive with insects and

44:32.660 --> 44:35.300
birds that live in the trees near the green water.

44:36.520 --> 44:41.030
Salties, he thinks, and he laughs. He does not

44:41.080 --> 44:48.030
believe the brothers, not for a moment. But just to be safe he pulls his new gun from the bag and

44:48.080 --> 44:51.520
checks it. Then he pulls a box from the bag,

44:52.040 --> 44:54.720
opens it carefully and looks at the diamonds.

44:55.860 --> 45:02.750
Beautiful, he thinks. Beautiful. And they belong to me. And

45:02.800 --> 45:04.400
he is about to laugh again

45:05.060 --> 45:12.650
when he hears a noise from the trees. The box shuts in an instant and then the gun is in

45:12.700 --> 45:13.070
his hand.

45:13.120 --> 45:18.280
He thinks he sees a dark shape but he does not shoot.

45:19.200 --> 45:26.600
Who's there? He says. But there is no answer. He picks the bag up and puts it on his shoulder.

45:27.540 --> 45:33.480
He takes a few steps towards the trees. I've got a gun. But there is silence.

45:34.800 --> 45:42.380
He tries to see what the dark shape is but the evening light is too weak. He moves a little closer.

45:43.120 --> 45:49.360
Maybe it's the brothers, he thinks. And he remembers the way one of them looked at the bag.

45:50.200 --> 45:57.610
Maybe they want to steal his bag. Well, they can try. He raises his gun and he's about to shoot

45:57.660 --> 46:02.930
when the dark shape moves again. And he laughs. It's

46:02.980 --> 46:06.520
just a small crocodile. A tiny thing.

46:07.340 --> 46:11.620
No bigger than his arm. He looks at it and laughs again.

46:12.460 --> 46:17.360
Well, you can't chop me in a little piece, can you? He says to the crocodile.

46:18.040 --> 46:21.040
And that is when he feels the knife at his neck.

46:21.780 --> 46:27.280
No, says a terrifyingly familiar voice. But I can, Jerry.

46:28.140 --> 46:34.020
Drop the gun. Jerry drops it and slowly turns. The knife still at his neck.

46:34.840 --> 46:41.510
Big Jones. But ow. Big Jones smiles. He is tall

46:41.560 --> 46:43.320
and extremely muscly.

46:44.060 --> 46:51.280
His face is bearded and his eyes are full of violence and pain. But it's his teeth, Jerry hates.

46:52.240 --> 46:58.170
Big white teeth as sharp as crocodiles. Ow. Do you

46:58.220 --> 47:00.440
think you can steal from me an escape?

47:01.260 --> 47:06.240
Jerry, you know me better than that. Please, Big Jones,

47:06.520 --> 47:13.900
I'm sorry. I have all the diamonds right here. Can't you let me go? You won't ever see me again, I promise. Jerry begs.

47:14.800 --> 47:21.020
I know I won't, Jerry. Because you're going to go on a little trip. All around the world.

47:21.960 --> 47:27.290
And Jerry remembers the old factory on the Thames and the boxes with the different addresses and

47:27.340 --> 47:31.200
the story about what Big Jones does when people steal from him.

47:32.240 --> 47:38.480
And Jerry opens his mouth to scream. But the screen he hears is not his own.

47:39.320 --> 47:46.310
The scream comes from Big Jones, who suddenly lets go of the knife. Jerry looks down and sees

47:46.360 --> 47:54.140
the small crocodile biting Big Jones's leg. Ha! Jerry laughs and he runs to his gun and

47:54.190 --> 47:58.590
points it at Big Jones, who falls to the floor and tries to kick the crocodile.

47:59.270 --> 48:02.650
Don't move, Big Jones. Let it chop you in a little pieces.

48:03.690 --> 48:08.640
And Big Jones looks up at him and is about to shout when suddenly he looks behind Jerry

48:08.690 --> 48:13.770
and the color disappears from his face. What? Says Jerry.

48:14.650 --> 48:17.010
And this is the last thing he ever says.

48:17.890 --> 48:24.030
Jerry's gun fires three times into the evening light and screams fill the creek,

48:24.890 --> 48:29.610
but there is no escape. Soon, all is quiet again,

48:30.350 --> 48:36.940
apart from the sound of three large crocodiles slowly creeping back to the green water. Their big

48:36.990 --> 48:39.130
mouths now full of dinner.

48:40.390 --> 48:43.990
And of sixty thousand pounds of uncut diamonds.

48:47.010 --> 48:53.680
The wrong bag. The sky is not there. In its

48:53.730 --> 48:56.800
place are a thousand adverts on a hundred

48:56.850 --> 49:04.110
television screens. A sea of lights that constantly move and change. Red, white,

49:04.430 --> 49:08.810
blue, green, orange, gold. Red, white, blue, green, orange, gold.

49:09.970 --> 49:10.810
They never stop.

49:11.790 --> 49:18.170
And on the television screens there are giant faces, clips from movies, news headlines,

49:18.630 --> 49:26.500
commercials for cars and perfume, chocolate and beer. The sounds never stop. The horns of

49:26.550 --> 49:29.640
the big yellow taxis, the music from the bars and

49:29.690 --> 49:34.810
the restaurants. And the voices. So many voices.

49:35.910 --> 49:43.870
So many that they are impossible to understand. And so many languages. English, Spanish,

49:44.390 --> 49:51.900
Chinese, Italian, German, Portuguese. They all mix together and are little more than noise to

49:51.950 --> 49:58.880
an onlooker. But the smells are the worst. One moment it's the scent of hot dogs, onions,

49:58.930 --> 50:05.390
tomatoes, fries or donuts. Delicious smells that make the stomach rumble.

50:06.430 --> 50:13.640
But then it's cigarettes, alcohol, garbage and vomit. And all of this mixes with the sweat of

50:13.690 --> 50:18.120
the thousands of people in one place. Jake begins to

50:18.170 --> 50:28.660
feel sick again and looks down at his dirty

50:28.710 --> 50:35.210
He always calls Jake Kidd. But he must be the same age as him, 12 or 13.

50:36.210 --> 50:40.820
It's okay. Jake says and looks up again. He has

50:40.870 --> 50:44.700
to be stronger, he knows. He has to be more like

50:44.750 --> 50:51.490
Nick. You're just hungry. After we eat, you will think this place is the best in the world.

50:52.470 --> 50:58.600
With a full belly, you can sit and watch this all day. It's just, well, why don't we come at

50:58.650 --> 51:06.090
night? Isn't it better at night, Nick? How dumb are you? His new friend says and he looks annoyed.

51:07.230 --> 51:11.300
At night they worry. At night everyone worries in New

51:11.350 --> 51:14.790
York. In the day when it's hot and sunny,

51:15.130 --> 51:20.770
like today, they don't worry about anything. They just watch those big screens take pictures,

51:21.150 --> 51:23.730
eat and drink. They don't even look at us twice.

51:25.070 --> 51:26.790
And Jake knows that he's right.

51:27.450 --> 51:30.430
Look, it's easy. You do what I say, no problem.

51:31.610 --> 51:33.190
And Nick looks at him for a moment.

51:34.230 --> 51:39.020
You look okay now. These new clothes aren't bad. The

51:39.070 --> 51:43.110
new clothes are not new, Jake knows. Not really.

51:44.110 --> 51:49.960
Nick steals clothes from the washing lines on the back streets. But they are better than the

51:50.010 --> 51:54.990
dirty old jeans and green jacket that are now in a bin behind the shop where they sit.

51:55.730 --> 52:02.310
You have to look good, kid. Remember that. You have to look like them, he says.

52:03.170 --> 52:06.250
Then they don't worry. You can get near to them.

52:06.710 --> 52:09.090
Be casual. Look up at the big screens.

52:09.310 --> 52:12.610
It's good if you get a camera out and pretend to take some snaps, you know.

52:13.230 --> 52:20.360
Then when you get the chance, you do it. You grab a bag. A woman's bag is the best. They have more

52:20.410 --> 52:26.990
things in them. But a guy's bag is easier. A guy's bag you can just walk away with.

52:27.850 --> 52:33.390
If you get a woman's bag, you sometimes have to run. Jake feels really sick now.

52:34.230 --> 52:37.540
The sun is somewhere up there above the skyscrapers of

52:37.590 --> 52:41.030
Times Square. And the afternoon is hot.

52:42.070 --> 52:49.500
The smells and sounds make a horrible mixture. He thinks about home for a moment. But that is

52:49.550 --> 52:55.970
too far away now and too long ago. You want to eat, don't you, kid? Jake nods.

52:56.990 --> 53:00.590
And you remember where the cameras are? The NYPD ones?

53:01.270 --> 53:02.630
The others are nothing.

53:03.470 --> 53:08.950
But don't let the NYPD ones see you or you're not coming back here, not with me. Jake nods again.

53:09.830 --> 53:12.080
So we do it now and we meet back on

53:12.130 --> 53:17.350
9th Avenue. You got it? Nick says. They both stand up.

53:18.010 --> 53:21.810
But before Nick moves into the crowds, Jake stops him.

53:22.630 --> 53:27.510
But what if someone sees me? What if they stop me? Nick smiles.

53:28.290 --> 53:36.240
No worries, just say it's the wrong bag. And then he goes. And Jake is alone. He moves out into

53:36.290 --> 53:43.370
the crowds of people. He knows where he has to go to the red stairs. He moves slowly.

53:44.250 --> 53:51.130
No one really sees him. No one really looks. But he sees. He looks. He watches.

53:52.210 --> 53:58.860
He sees kids his own age laughing and joking. He looks at the food on the small tables in front

53:58.910 --> 54:05.110
of the cafes and bars. He sees two policemen in the distance and he moves the other way,

54:05.570 --> 54:12.520
walking between families and couples, businessmen and tourists. When he reaches the red steps,

54:12.570 --> 54:18.430
he is hot and sweaty, hungry too. But you can't think about that, he tells himself.

54:19.510 --> 54:25.910
He needs to think clearly now. He needs to think like Nick. On the red steps,

54:26.290 --> 54:32.430
there is a group of maybe 30 Japanese tourists. They all have a camera and are all taking photos.

54:33.530 --> 54:37.550
But their bags are on their shoulders and there are too many of them, he knows.

54:38.310 --> 54:43.290
Most do not see anything, but one always sees something.

54:44.110 --> 54:47.110
And one is enough. So who?

54:48.190 --> 54:56.010
And then he sees the man. He looks like any other foreign tourist. He has a camera around his neck,

54:56.590 --> 54:59.270
sunglasses on his face and a map in his hand.

55:00.170 --> 55:02.810
Except Jake is not sure he is a tourist.

55:03.430 --> 55:09.800
There is something different about him. The man does not use the camera and does not look at the

55:09.850 --> 55:16.390
map. In fact, the man does nothing at all. Occasionally he just looks at his watch.

55:17.510 --> 55:21.510
But the bag is by his feet. It looks expensive,

55:22.490 --> 55:23.770
made from black leather.

55:24.630 --> 55:31.100
Nick calls them weekend bags. He says they are the best. Inside them there are wallets,

55:31.150 --> 55:33.450
phones, tablets, everything.

55:34.310 --> 55:41.530
Okay. Okay, you can do this. Jake tells himself. He just needs to be quick.

55:42.470 --> 55:48.070
The man never even looks at the bag, so Jake begins to move in his direction casually.

55:48.910 --> 55:51.450
He looks up at the lights and the television screens.

55:52.530 --> 55:58.520
Then he is near enough and he looks at the man. He thinks again that he is a strange

55:58.570 --> 56:06.020
tourist. From here Jake can see that he looks nervous. But then the man looks to the right and

56:06.070 --> 56:13.690
Jake knows it is his best chance. He grabs the bag and moves away. He expects to hear a shout,

56:14.510 --> 56:22.080
but there is nothing. So he moves into the crowds like Nick does. He wants to run, but he walks

56:22.130 --> 56:25.710
and notices that the bag is heavy, really heavy.

56:27.170 --> 56:33.020
And he begins to imagine Nick's face when he sees the bag and he already begins to imagine

56:33.070 --> 56:36.300
the food that he can buy. But the bag is

56:36.350 --> 56:41.290
heavy, he thinks again. So heavy. And he slows.

56:41.970 --> 56:48.500
Maybe he should look inside, he thinks, just to make sure. He looks around and steps into the

56:48.550 --> 56:55.840
doorway of a shop for a moment. Then he kneels down and opens the bag. And for a moment he does

56:55.890 --> 57:00.930
not understand. There is no wallet, no phone, no tablet,

57:01.350 --> 57:03.930
no clothes. There is only.

57:04.950 --> 57:11.040
Then he hears the man shout and he looks up and sees his angry face running towards him through

57:11.090 --> 57:18.270
the crowd. He has no time to think, but he must. Can he leave the bag? Can he just run away?

57:19.450 --> 57:23.010
Run away? When he knows what the man is going to do?

57:24.970 --> 57:31.490
No. Not this time. And then he is up and runs too and he has the bag in his hands.

57:32.450 --> 57:38.800
For a moment he has no idea what to do or where to go. He thinks that maybe Nick would know that

57:38.850 --> 57:43.850
Nick would have some advice. And then he realises that he does know.

57:44.910 --> 57:48.490
And you remember where the cameras are? The NYPD ones?

57:49.410 --> 57:55.330
Yes, I remember Jake thinks. But can he get there before the man catches him?

57:56.210 --> 57:59.770
There is another shout from behind him, but Jake keeps moving.

58:00.570 --> 58:07.520
Above the crowd he can see the NYPD camera on top of a tall post. And he pushes past the

58:07.570 --> 58:12.210
tourists to reach it. For a moment there are people all around him.

58:12.950 --> 58:18.420
And he thinks he will never get there. But then he pushes past some kids who shout at him and

58:18.470 --> 58:23.340
he is there, directly below the camera. And now? Now

58:23.390 --> 58:29.480
what? He hears another shout and knows that the man is nearly here. But the camera is not

58:29.530 --> 58:37.170
looking at him. It is turning slowly in the other direction. Hey! He shouts and he begins to jump up and down.

58:37.830 --> 58:42.910
Down here! Look down here! But he has no idea if anyone can hear him.

58:43.430 --> 58:47.890
He opens the bag for everyone to see and there is a cry from a woman near to him,

58:48.290 --> 58:55.060
followed by another and another. This must be why the camera turns. Jake holds up the bag,

58:55.110 --> 58:59.870
but he is scared, so scared, though he has to do it.

59:00.550 --> 59:05.970
That's mine. The man's voice says behind him. And there are more cries from the crowd,

59:06.250 --> 59:08.590
and everyone seems to know what is happening now.

59:09.190 --> 59:14.250
Give it to me, the man says. Jake looks at the bag one more time.

59:15.190 --> 59:18.230
What he sees inside looks just like from the movies.

59:19.170 --> 59:26.860
The clock, the wires, and the heavy lights to give it to the man because he is

59:26.910 --> 59:31.330
so scared. But he will not. No, he answers.

59:32.130 --> 59:37.700
Then someone in the crowd says something and the man looks around. The scared cries now

59:37.750 --> 59:44.540
become angry shouts. Jake is pushed and he falls and loses the bag. He thinks that the

59:44.590 --> 59:46.850
people are shouting at him and he wants to explain.

59:47.630 --> 59:49.420
But it was not the man who pushed

59:49.470 --> 59:56.790
Jake and took the bag. It was a policeman. The man is underneath four NYPD officers.

59:57.650 --> 01:00:02.570
Jake now realises that the crowd is not shouting. It is cheering.

01:00:03.550 --> 01:00:07.870
And a police officer helps him to stand and says something about him being a hero.

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:13.890
How did you know, kid? A voice says. But this time it's not Nick.

01:00:14.850 --> 01:00:21.250
I didn't, he manages to say. It's just the wrong bag.

01:00:22.510 --> 01:00:29.050
The perfect murder. The QM2 darling is the finest ocean liner in the world.

01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:34.750
Sure, there are one or two bigger than her but none of them has her romance or her luxury.

01:00:35.950 --> 01:00:39.690
Mrs Carolina Heath says as they enter the dining room.

01:00:40.490 --> 01:00:43.050
Her young new friend only smiles.

01:00:43.890 --> 01:00:47.980
She is a quiet young thing and beautiful too. And

01:00:48.030 --> 01:00:51.010
Carolina likes to have beautiful things around her.

01:00:51.690 --> 01:00:54.290
So she is more than happy to talk for both of them.

01:00:54.770 --> 01:01:01.230
This is your first cruise, you say? You're going to love it. Just look at the view out there.

01:01:02.090 --> 01:01:07.560
And from the large glass windows they can see the majestic figure of the Statue of Liberty

01:01:07.610 --> 01:01:11.040
as the ship slowly moves away from the port of

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:13.850
New York and heads into the deep of the ocean.

01:01:15.150 --> 01:01:21.850
It's fantastic, Eleanor says. And again Carolina is very happy to have a new companion.

01:01:23.210 --> 01:01:30.490
The dining room is magnificent. The tables are covered in white and the lights are low and atmospheric.

01:01:31.830 --> 01:01:35.130
The band in the corner is playing soft classical music.

01:01:35.910 --> 01:01:38.290
Well I wonder what the other people at our table are like.

01:01:39.010 --> 01:01:41.170
Oh my look, here we are, table six.

01:01:42.190 --> 01:01:48.850
Well good evening gentlemen, Carolina says. And the three men stop their conversation and smile.

01:01:49.770 --> 01:01:53.490
Mrs Heath, one says, how good to see you again.

01:01:54.270 --> 01:01:56.550
Another evening of champagne and dancing?

01:01:57.550 --> 01:02:00.970
Every night Peter, my doctor tells me that I must.

01:02:01.710 --> 01:02:02.190
She laughs.

01:02:02.890 --> 01:02:06.160
Gentlemen, let me introduce you to my new friend, Miss

01:02:06.210 --> 01:02:06.930
Eleanor Chance.

01:02:07.910 --> 01:02:13.870
Eleanor only nods and smiles. And these Eleanor, well how to describe them?

01:02:14.290 --> 01:02:20.270
I suppose every ship needs some pirates. And these are they. Meet Peter, Edward and Michael.

01:02:21.250 --> 01:02:28.080
The men stand and nod politely. And Carolina is amused by the interested looks they give Eleanor

01:02:28.130 --> 01:02:35.300
Chance. Pirates, Eleanor asks. Well maybe not pirates. But you

01:02:35.350 --> 01:02:36.310
should still be careful.

01:02:37.150 --> 01:02:40.470
They are in fact writers. And I don't know which is worse.

01:02:41.250 --> 01:02:48.900
Writers, how interesting. Please sit down. Edward says with a smile that shows early signs of

01:02:48.950 --> 01:02:54.920
infatuation. No I can't, I must find the captain. He promised to tell me all about the new people

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:58.180
on board today. You know how I must know who

01:02:58.230 --> 01:03:00.890
I'm sailing with. She looks at the men.

01:03:01.710 --> 01:03:04.080
Oh but can I trust you three to look after

01:03:04.130 --> 01:03:07.290
Eleanor? This is her first night and her first cruise.

01:03:08.250 --> 01:03:12.650
She does not need to hear any of your horrible stories or strange ideas.

01:03:13.570 --> 01:03:18.830
Oh well I suppose we can change the subject. But we are having a really interesting conversation.

01:03:19.610 --> 01:03:23.990
Carolina laughs. I don't want to know. Just be nice.

01:03:25.050 --> 01:03:26.530
And she smiles at Eleanor.

01:03:27.470 --> 01:03:32.850
If they scare you just scream and I can rescue you. Oh I think I can look after myself.

01:03:33.530 --> 01:03:38.950
responds Eleanor. And again Carolina thinks what a sweet lovely thing she is.

01:03:39.630 --> 01:03:43.900
She must be only 25 or 26. Her long blonde

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:47.690
hair is elegant and her face full of classical beauty.

01:03:48.430 --> 01:03:54.400
And those eyes. Such innocent blue eyes. Yes a lovely

01:03:54.450 --> 01:03:56.890
new companion she thinks as she leaves.

01:03:57.850 --> 01:04:02.490
At the table Edward offers Eleanor a chair and Michael passes her a glass of champagne.

01:04:03.550 --> 01:04:08.450
So are you all writers? She asks. Yes says Peter.

01:04:09.270 --> 01:04:16.880
Of different types. I'm in film Edward here as a novelist and Michael writes plays. Fascinating. She

01:04:16.930 --> 01:04:22.920
says. And all three look happy that she thinks so. And what is this interesting conversation

01:04:22.970 --> 01:04:28.460
that I'm interrupting? Oh we can't bore you with that. Please tell us about yourself says

01:04:28.510 --> 01:04:28.750
Edward.

01:04:29.730 --> 01:04:35.240
Eleanor smiles shyly and moves the elegant white silk scarf that lies around her neck

01:04:35.290 --> 01:04:38.780
above her black dress. There is very little to say

01:04:38.830 --> 01:04:43.470
I'm sailing from here to Southampton then I intend to stay in England for some time.

01:04:44.230 --> 01:04:45.210
For the summer perhaps.

01:04:46.290 --> 01:04:50.910
Then she is quiet and the three men think how polite and gentle she seems.

01:04:51.750 --> 01:04:58.650
I see says Peter. Well this is a tradition of ours. Every year we take a cruise on old Mary.

01:04:59.490 --> 01:05:03.540
This is the sixth year. A good escape from editors and deadlines.

01:05:03.590 --> 01:05:09.060
And wives says Michael. Well for them not for me

01:05:09.110 --> 01:05:12.470
I'm not married says Edward. A little too quickly.

01:05:13.410 --> 01:05:16.800
For a moment there's an awkward silence and Peter and

01:05:16.850 --> 01:05:18.250
Michael exchange a smile.

01:05:18.910 --> 01:05:23.510
Well our conversation is about murder says Peter to fill the silence.

01:05:24.370 --> 01:05:30.320
The perfect murder. Eleanor smiles. I see. I'm sure she's

01:05:30.370 --> 01:05:32.410
not interested Pete says Edward.

01:05:33.150 --> 01:05:38.250
Oh but I am. Mrs. Heath is lovely but she only talks about scandal and gossip.

01:05:39.230 --> 01:05:46.880
See Edward? We can continue to argue. Michael jokes. You see we all have very different ideas

01:05:46.930 --> 01:05:52.660
about what the perfect murder is. Peter thinks that for a film there must be something visual

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:58.130
about the murder weapon. For me the important things are the character and the motive.

01:05:59.150 --> 01:06:03.130
And for me Edward says as he refills Eleanor's glass.

01:06:04.250 --> 01:06:05.850
The important thing is the twist.

01:06:06.750 --> 01:06:11.710
The twist. You know the surprise at the end of the story the thing you don't expect.

01:06:12.650 --> 01:06:18.430
Eleanor smiles and takes a drink. The statue of liberty is still visible in the distance

01:06:19.010 --> 01:06:21.290
but they will soon be alone on the dark ocean.

01:06:22.330 --> 01:06:28.590
You know I think you're all correct. In fact I have an idea for a story. An idea I think about occasionally.

01:06:29.230 --> 01:06:32.290
Really? Says Peter. Well you must tell us.

01:06:33.110 --> 01:06:37.090
Maybe she says with another shy smile. But you first.

01:06:37.790 --> 01:06:43.820
What's your idea for the perfect murder weapon? Peter laughs. Okay well it's not my idea and

01:06:43.870 --> 01:06:45.990
it's old but I think it's great for a film.

01:06:46.770 --> 01:06:53.210
It's an icicle. The other two men laugh. He always says this. Edward informs Eleanor.

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:58.460
Because it's perfect. After the murderer kills the victim they just watch the

01:06:58.510 --> 01:07:03.040
weapon melt. No fingerprints. No evidence. What do you think

01:07:03.090 --> 01:07:04.470
Eleanor? She smiles.

01:07:05.470 --> 01:07:11.480
It's very clever. But well I prefer fire. Fire. Yes

01:07:11.530 --> 01:07:14.430
fire kills and it destroys the evidence.

01:07:15.350 --> 01:07:19.190
And also a fire can look like an accident. The icicle never can.

01:07:20.030 --> 01:07:26.970
Haha she's right. Laughs Michael. So what about characters and motive? He continues.

01:07:27.650 --> 01:07:30.710
A beautiful young couple who are happy and in love.

01:07:31.550 --> 01:07:34.590
What and he kills her? No she kills him.

01:07:35.390 --> 01:07:39.910
Let me guess they're rich. She wants his money. Eleanor takes another drink.

01:07:40.830 --> 01:07:45.510
No she's the rich one. So what's the motive? Michael asks.

01:07:46.110 --> 01:07:50.650
Wait a minute. Interrupts Peter. You say she kills him.

01:07:51.510 --> 01:07:53.030
With fire. How?

01:07:53.750 --> 01:07:55.930
She locks him in a room in a burning house?

01:07:56.770 --> 01:07:58.470
No because then he might escape.

01:07:59.290 --> 01:08:04.610
She drugs him with sleeping pills during dinner. She always goes for a run in the evening.

01:08:05.450 --> 01:08:09.110
But this time she waits until he falls asleep in a chair in the lounge.

01:08:09.870 --> 01:08:16.000
And she puts a candle to the curtains. Then she goes for her run and makes sure that many people

01:08:16.050 --> 01:08:23.440
see her. Peter nods his head. Interesting but a coroner will find traces of sleeping pills in

01:08:23.490 --> 01:08:29.500
the blood. They'll know. No not with this type of pill. At a certain temperature it completely

01:08:29.550 --> 01:08:36.210
burns away. Only a small trace of it can be detected. And if they are detected. So what?

01:08:36.770 --> 01:08:44.530
They are his pills. Peter laughs this time. Excellent. Very clever. Edward agrees.

01:08:45.210 --> 01:08:48.130
But unfortunately the wife is always a suspect in a murder.

01:08:49.030 --> 01:08:55.690
Normally yes says Eleanor. And all three men notice how her smile fades a little.

01:08:56.550 --> 01:09:01.470
But she has a plan for that. You see she runs until she hears the fire engines.

01:09:02.230 --> 01:09:07.070
Then she returns to the house. There are people there already. Neighbors.

01:09:07.570 --> 01:09:13.610
And this is just what she wants. They try to stop her but she runs into the burning building.

01:09:14.330 --> 01:09:21.000
No one follows her. They can't. They know it's too dangerous. And so she goes to her husband as

01:09:21.050 --> 01:09:27.540
the flames spread over his body and she watches. And while she watches she lets the flames touch

01:09:27.590 --> 01:09:32.510
her too. They reach out and they burn her back and her neck and her hair.

01:09:33.410 --> 01:09:37.270
And at this point she screams and runs from the building and falls to the floor.

01:09:38.530 --> 01:09:43.290
Eleanor stops speaking. And for a moment there is silence at the table.

01:09:44.630 --> 01:09:48.750
My god says Edward. She lets the flames burn her.

01:09:49.910 --> 01:09:56.970
Michael takes a moment to think about it and then nods. It's perfect. The perfect way to avoid suspicion.

01:09:58.110 --> 01:10:02.290
Everyone thinks it's an accident and that she is heartbroken says Peter.

01:10:03.070 --> 01:10:08.390
Yes. Eleanor says. And for a moment more there is silence again.

01:10:09.770 --> 01:10:16.250
Then the band starts to play a waltz and Eleanor looks at Edward. I don't suppose you dance Edward.

01:10:17.190 --> 01:10:20.210
And the writer goes a little red. Actually I do.

01:10:20.790 --> 01:10:21.750
Would you like to join me?

01:10:22.150 --> 01:10:28.070
Eleanor smiles and they both stand up. She adjusts the white scarf around her neck.

01:10:28.790 --> 01:10:33.170
But what about the woman's motive? Michael asks. Eleanor smiles.

01:10:34.070 --> 01:10:35.610
Yes that's the difficult part.

01:10:36.370 --> 01:10:42.330
And I'm afraid I just don't know. I suppose sometimes a person just enjoys murder.

01:10:43.590 --> 01:10:49.180
And as Eleanor and Edward move away to the dance floor Peter and Michael see her adjust the

01:10:49.230 --> 01:10:54.480
scarf around her neck one last time. And in the low romantic light of the ballroom

01:10:54.530 --> 01:11:00.690
they both think they see something strange on her back and neck. Something which makes them stop

01:11:01.150 --> 01:11:08.680
and look at each other. But no. It cannot be what they think it is. It cannot be burnt skin

01:11:08.730 --> 01:11:16.170
under the elegant white scarf. Can it? Case closed.

01:11:19.610 --> 01:11:27.080
Normally Nick likes his job but not today. Today he just wants to get out of the hot

01:11:27.130 --> 01:11:33.520
courtroom away from the serious lawyers in their black gowns and white wigs and go and enjoy the

01:11:33.570 --> 01:11:40.760
son. It's a good job he thinks. It's better than serving drinks in a pub or helping out at his dad's

01:11:40.810 --> 01:11:47.840
office. But only if it's a short day and only if the case is closed quickly. But today it's nearly

01:11:47.890 --> 01:11:53.550
three and the case is nowhere near closed. Still he likes the title of his job.

01:11:54.490 --> 01:12:01.680
Court reporter sounds good. It sounds important. And that is exactly how he describes it to any

01:12:01.730 --> 01:12:08.700
girl that asks. In reality however all he does is press play on the recording device and write

01:12:08.750 --> 01:12:14.400
down a few notes about when the judge enters, when the prosecution or defense speaks or what the

01:12:14.450 --> 01:12:20.340
final verdict is. And sometimes the cases are interesting. And he likes having some good

01:12:20.390 --> 01:12:26.070
stories to tell the guys in the pub. He also enjoys deciding who is innocent and who is guilty.

01:12:26.870 --> 01:12:33.540
But today that's too easy. So he looks out of the window and waits for the judge. Sometimes he

01:12:33.590 --> 01:12:40.640
looks across at the defendant's sister, a young woman with long dark hair and sad eyes. But only

01:12:40.690 --> 01:12:44.650
sometimes because the defendant and the rest of her family,

01:12:44.890 --> 01:12:52.020
the Lee family, are wild. And he does not want to make her or them angry. They have

01:12:52.070 --> 01:12:59.130
a terrible reputation in Bristol and there is not one of them that does not scare Nick. All rise.

01:13:00.150 --> 01:13:07.890
The court clerk says. And the jury, the lawyers and the family of the defendant all stand. Nick does too.

01:13:08.330 --> 01:13:15.030
And he remembers to press the button on the device. He forgot to do it once and they refused to pay him for the day.

01:13:15.990 --> 01:13:19.140
Please be seated, says the judge, a woman of about

01:13:19.190 --> 01:13:22.230
40 years of age with a serious face and tone.

01:13:23.110 --> 01:13:30.180
My summary of this case is brief. The jury know all the details. On Wednesday the 5th of January

01:13:30.230 --> 01:13:35.780
Mrs. Dawson went to the house of her sister, the defendant, Miss Lee. Mrs. Dawson was bleeding

01:13:35.830 --> 01:13:42.660
from her nose and mouth. She also had marks on her face. She says her husband, the deceased

01:13:42.710 --> 01:13:48.720
Mr. Dawson, hit her there several times. There are pictures of these injuries but there is no

01:13:48.770 --> 01:13:55.420
proof that Mr. Dawson hit Mrs. Dawson. However, that is not why you are here. You are here because

01:13:55.470 --> 01:14:00.220
when Miss Lee saw her young sister, she took a pair of scissors from her kitchen table,

01:14:00.270 --> 01:14:05.190
said something to her sister and left the house. The words, she said, were,

01:14:05.530 --> 01:14:09.690
I will kill him. But the defendant says she does not remember that.

01:14:10.270 --> 01:14:13.940
Several people saw the defendant walking around Bristol, looking for

01:14:13.990 --> 01:14:14.430
Mr. Dawson.

01:14:15.330 --> 01:14:18.980
She stopped twice. Once at the house of one of

01:14:19.030 --> 01:14:21.910
Mrs. Dawson's friends and once at a local pub,

01:14:22.710 --> 01:14:27.880
both times she had the scissors with her and both times witnesses testified that she said,

01:14:27.930 --> 01:14:34.200
I will kill him. At half past two in the afternoon on the 5th of January, Miss Stella Lee found

01:14:34.250 --> 01:14:34.730
Mr.

01:14:34.950 --> 01:14:42.200
Dawson. He was leaving a cafe with a friend. The friend, Mr. Harris, says that Miss Lee did not say

01:14:42.250 --> 01:14:45.730
anything to Mr. Dawson and that the only thing Mr.

01:14:45.970 --> 01:14:49.460
Dawson said was hello. Miss Lee then stabbed

01:14:49.510 --> 01:14:56.860
Mr. Dawson in the neck with the scissors and he died in seconds due to loss of blood. Miss Lee

01:14:56.910 --> 01:15:02.780
waited until the police came and was arrested. Ladies and gentlemen of the jury, you must decide

01:15:02.830 --> 01:15:10.460
only one thing. The defense says that this is not murder, but manslaughter. They say this is the

01:15:10.510 --> 01:15:16.340
case because Miss Lee was not in a sane state of mind when she killed Mr. Dawson. They say that

01:15:16.390 --> 01:15:22.940
this crime was in defense of her sister. The prosecution says this is not true. They say this

01:15:22.990 --> 01:15:27.470
is premeditated murder because for half an hour Miss Lee looked for Mr. Dawson.

01:15:28.430 --> 01:15:34.320
They also say she made her intention clear on three different occasions. Please leave us now

01:15:34.370 --> 01:15:38.950
and take some time to consider these events. All rise.

01:15:39.870 --> 01:15:41.170
The court clerk says again,

01:15:42.110 --> 01:15:46.900
the judge leaves, the jury returns to its room to make a verdict and the Lee family

01:15:46.950 --> 01:15:53.930
exit to wait in the lobby. Nick sighs. It is obvious what the decision will be,

01:15:54.430 --> 01:15:59.790
but it looks like he will be here until four now. Cigarette time then, he thinks,

01:16:00.230 --> 01:16:06.840
and he leaves the courtroom. He has at least 10 minutes. No jury can make a decision in less

01:16:06.890 --> 01:16:12.900
than 10 minutes. He takes the back stairs, passes three or four lawyers in their black gowns and

01:16:12.950 --> 01:16:19.700
white wigs and feels sorry for them. It is so hot today. Then he pushes open a firedoor to his

01:16:19.750 --> 01:16:24.270
personal smoking area at the back of the court next to the bins and lights his cigarette.

01:16:25.410 --> 01:16:31.610
The feeling of the sun on his face is good and the taste of the smoke is even better.

01:16:32.810 --> 01:16:38.660
Tonight is a good night to go out for a few drinks, he thinks, and he is deciding whom to call when

01:16:38.710 --> 01:16:43.570
he hears a female voice. Yeah, everything is fine, perfect.

01:16:44.450 --> 01:16:46.310
No, no one has an idea,

01:16:46.890 --> 01:16:54.210
not the police, not my mental family. Of course it will be murder. What? Oh, who cares about her?

01:16:55.010 --> 01:17:01.220
She will probably like prison the crazy cow, more people for her to work. And then I get his money

01:17:01.270 --> 01:17:07.300
and you and I can get the L out of this city and out of this country. Nick walks to the bins and

01:17:07.350 --> 01:17:13.720
looks around them. On the other side is the sister of the defendant. In the courtroom she always

01:17:13.770 --> 01:17:18.670
looks sad and confused. Now, however, she looks only victorious.

01:17:19.970 --> 01:17:26.420
Jesus, Nick says before he can stop himself. And the girl sees him and closes the

01:17:26.470 --> 01:17:32.780
mobile phone in her hand. Nick looks at her for a second and then turns and runs back to the

01:17:32.830 --> 01:17:36.960
door. He walks back up the stairs feeling confused and

01:17:37.010 --> 01:17:41.670
uncertain. He has to say something to someone, but who?

01:17:42.670 --> 01:17:46.800
The judge? Maybe. He pushes the door to

01:17:46.850 --> 01:17:53.180
the court from open. No one is there. He moves towards another door and then hears someone else

01:17:53.230 --> 01:18:00.640
enter the court. Wait, Mrs. Dawson says, and she looks like she might cry. Please wait, I need to

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:05.720
explain. Nick waits. Why not? What can she do to

01:18:05.770 --> 01:18:12.460
him in here? She stands near to him and he can see the tears in her eyes and the sad expression

01:18:12.510 --> 01:18:16.270
on her face. But this time he knows it's not real.

01:18:17.130 --> 01:18:24.240
I heard you. Your own sister. You wanted her to kill your husband. No, you don't understand.

01:18:24.290 --> 01:18:30.880
I don't know what you were, but I loved my sister and I used to love my husband. For a second

01:18:30.930 --> 01:18:35.120
only, Nick waits. Then he shakes his head and is

01:18:35.170 --> 01:18:39.280
about to turn. Look, she says, her voice different

01:18:39.330 --> 01:18:46.310
now. I saw you looking at me yesterday. Maybe there's something we can do to make you forget this?

01:18:47.230 --> 01:18:53.260
Maybe you can take me for a drink. Then she smiles and takes his hand. For a second,

01:18:53.310 --> 01:19:00.420
he does not know what to say. But he knows what to do. And he pulls his hand away and moves to the

01:19:00.470 --> 01:19:08.220
door. For a second, she is silent. But then she speaks again. Though this time her voice is hard

01:19:08.270 --> 01:19:11.710
and angry. Fine, go and tell them what you were.

01:19:12.210 --> 01:19:19.560
Do you have any proof? No, so you can tell them what you want, but they can do nothing. And you

01:19:19.610 --> 01:19:22.870
are still here in Bristol. And so is my family.

01:19:23.590 --> 01:19:30.750
And they will not like what you say. Do you understand me? Nick stops. He understands.

01:19:32.050 --> 01:19:39.000
And she's right. There's nothing he can do. That's right, little boy. Keep quiet and you're

01:19:39.050 --> 01:19:42.650
going to be okay. I never hurt anyone. She did.

01:19:43.370 --> 01:19:46.600
Is it my fault she's so stupid? So easy to manipulate.

01:19:46.650 --> 01:19:54.080
A little bit of blood on my face. A few tears. A fake story about him hitting me. It was

01:19:54.130 --> 01:19:54.750
so easy.

01:19:55.450 --> 01:20:00.350
And if you tell anyone, it's going to be just as easy to finish you. Understand?

01:20:01.490 --> 01:20:05.130
Then the door opens and the lawyers return. And Mrs.

01:20:05.470 --> 01:20:07.310
Dawson's expression of sadness returns.

01:20:08.410 --> 01:20:13.260
Slowly they all come back in. And Nick knows he has to say something.

01:20:13.310 --> 01:20:19.310
He has to do something. But there's no time. And there's nothing he can do.

01:20:20.290 --> 01:20:25.590
The Lee family is back in the room now. And he looks at them. They are savage,

01:20:26.190 --> 01:20:32.860
terrible people who love violence. So he moves back to his desk and he sits down in the seat

01:20:32.910 --> 01:20:40.540
and feels defeated. He wants to say something. But without proof, he has nothing and the judge

01:20:40.590 --> 01:20:45.010
can do nothing. And he hears the door to the judge's chamber open.

01:20:46.150 --> 01:20:51.120
All rise. John says and Nick knows it's too late

01:20:51.170 --> 01:20:58.310
now. He stands and is about to press the button on the recording device when he sees the red light flashing.

01:20:59.170 --> 01:21:02.620
The red light. The red light means

01:21:02.670 --> 01:21:11.890
it's recording. It's recording. It was recording. It recorded. Stop,

01:21:12.490 --> 01:21:13.010
Nick shouts.

01:21:14.190 --> 01:21:16.850
And he hands the recording device to the court clerk.

01:21:17.810 --> 01:21:20.530
I think the judge needs to listen to this first.

01:21:21.370 --> 01:21:24.050
He says and he looks back at the beautiful Mrs.

01:21:24.370 --> 01:21:31.130
Dawson and sees that she is not sad now and that she is not victorious and not angry.

01:21:31.710 --> 01:21:37.870
And he smiles at her because he thinks this expression suits her best. Yes,

01:21:38.410 --> 01:21:42.570
he thinks Mrs. Dawson looks best when she is scared.

01:21:43.950 --> 01:21:44.910
Don't move.

01:21:52.010 --> 01:21:57.450
It feels like ice cold water. One moment you are asleep.

01:21:58.230 --> 01:22:03.470
Your dreams calm and gentle and your body warm in the blankets of your bed.

01:22:04.430 --> 01:22:09.610
The next moment you are awake. A horrible sensation filling your body.

01:22:10.410 --> 01:22:15.330
And you are sure that ice cold water is covering you. But it's not.

01:22:16.110 --> 01:22:22.570
No, it's something much worse. Something that makes your heart beat faster.

01:22:23.370 --> 01:22:28.620
Something that makes the hairs on your arms stand up and that makes your eyes search the

01:22:28.670 --> 01:22:36.110
horrible complete darkness of your room. No, it's not ice cold water. It's fear.

01:22:37.130 --> 01:22:43.240
And for a few moments you are so afraid that you cannot think. You know you are still in

01:22:43.290 --> 01:22:48.910
your bed, still in your house, still in your quiet street in the north of Manchester.

01:22:49.630 --> 01:22:54.830
And you want to tell yourself to relax. That everything is okay.

01:22:56.030 --> 01:23:02.290
And to close your eyes and return to your calm and gentle dreams. But you cannot.

01:23:03.130 --> 01:23:07.490
No, you cannot because you know that everything is not okay.

01:23:08.310 --> 01:23:11.590
You know that you are awake because something woke you.

01:23:12.290 --> 01:23:12.930
Some noise.

01:23:13.570 --> 01:23:19.170
Some movement. Somebody. And you try to remember what it was.

01:23:19.950 --> 01:23:25.870
But you cannot. So you lie there and you let your eyes adjust to the dark.

01:23:26.710 --> 01:23:32.410
And you do not tell yourself to relax. You do not tell yourself that everything is okay.

01:23:33.290 --> 01:23:37.390
And you do not tell yourself to return to your calm and gentle dreams.

01:23:38.010 --> 01:23:45.280
You tell yourself this. Don't move. The blanket of your bed is covering all of you

01:23:45.330 --> 01:23:49.230
except for your head. And part of you thinks that this is good.

01:23:49.970 --> 01:23:56.770
Part of you wants to cover your head too. To hide. To be completely silent and to wait.

01:23:57.670 --> 01:24:05.280
But another part of you wants to know. Needs to know. So you listen. And you look.

01:24:05.330 --> 01:24:11.390
And you try to remember what it was that woke you. A dog outside in the street.

01:24:12.370 --> 01:24:15.620
The alarm of a car. A baby crying in the

01:24:15.670 --> 01:24:21.060
next house. Or something closer. Something inside the

01:24:21.110 --> 01:24:29.070
house. The sound of breaking glass. The noise of the front door opening. A voice in the dark.

01:24:29.950 --> 01:24:37.430
So you do not move. But you let your eyes adjust. And soon the complete darkness is not so complete.

01:24:38.390 --> 01:24:43.400
And you can see your room now. Though everything seems strange and different. And

01:24:43.450 --> 01:24:49.900
everything makes you feel more afraid. The door of your wardrobe is open. And the clothes inside

01:24:49.950 --> 01:24:57.020
look like three or four small burglars watching you. The lamp in the corner of the room looks like

01:24:57.070 --> 01:25:03.320
the tall silent figure of a murderer. And the coat on the back of your door has hands that almost

01:25:03.370 --> 01:25:07.570
touch you. But this is just your imagination. Your fear.

01:25:08.130 --> 01:25:15.320
And you know that. And try to stay calm and focused. So you listen. And you think that everything

01:25:15.370 --> 01:25:22.360
seems to be normal. Inside the room everything is silent. Apart from the slow and constant tick

01:25:22.410 --> 01:25:24.030
of the small clock on the wall.

01:25:24.550 --> 01:25:31.200
And the fast irregular beat of your heart. You try to see the clock in the dark. But it's

01:25:31.250 --> 01:25:37.780
impossible. And you think that it must be somewhere between four and six o'clock. The darkest hours

01:25:37.830 --> 01:25:44.280
of the night when even the lively and vibrant city of Manchester sleeps. Yes outside the house

01:25:44.330 --> 01:25:50.540
the streets are silent. There are no shouts or loud voices coming from university students

01:25:50.590 --> 01:25:56.620
returning home from the pubs and bars. There is not yet the sound of the milk van or rubbish

01:25:56.670 --> 01:26:02.990
collectors. And there is no bird song to let you know that the sun and the new day are nearly here.

01:26:03.910 --> 01:26:09.550
You can imagine the street outside. Twenty terraced houses stretching down the road.

01:26:10.310 --> 01:26:14.350
Small houses with neither rich nor poor people inside them.

01:26:15.250 --> 01:26:17.300
All with tidy and ordinary gardens

01:26:17.350 --> 01:26:23.740
and ordinary cars. All silent and all dark. You think

01:26:23.790 --> 01:26:26.610
that if you scream someone will hear you.

01:26:27.190 --> 01:26:31.740
But you are not sure. Not completely sure. And so

01:26:31.790 --> 01:26:36.010
you wait. And you listen. And you look.

01:26:36.770 --> 01:26:43.000
And you tell yourself one more time. Don't move. After

01:26:43.050 --> 01:26:50.320
a few more moments you begin to feel a little more relaxed. The street is silent. And the house is

01:26:50.370 --> 01:26:57.670
too. Maybe it was only a bad dream that woke you. Maybe it was a text on your mobile phone.

01:26:58.510 --> 01:27:05.400
Maybe it was an animal in the garden. And then you think of Sophie. Your cat. But you know that

01:27:05.450 --> 01:27:11.240
the noise was not her. You know that Sophie is sleeping in her usual position at the top of the

01:27:11.290 --> 01:27:17.740
stairs. And that she never makes a noise in the night. And you wish for the first time that you

01:27:17.790 --> 01:27:25.720
also had a dog. A large brave dog to bark and growl at any intruders. Yes a brave dog to

01:27:25.770 --> 01:27:33.720
protect you instead of just a lazy cat that lies in dangerous places. And you almost smile. And you relax

01:27:33.770 --> 01:27:37.340
a little more. It was just a bad dream.

01:27:37.390 --> 01:27:44.590
You think. And your heart slows. And the sensation of ice cold fear begins to leave you.

01:27:45.170 --> 01:27:51.110
And now you move. You pull the covers down to your chest and make yourself comfortable again.

01:27:51.910 --> 01:27:58.960
And you know that you can rest now. Everything is okay. And your eyes close. And you feel sleep

01:27:59.010 --> 01:28:03.090
begin to take you. And then you hear it. There.

01:28:03.450 --> 01:28:07.380
A sound. Not a dream. But movement in the spare

01:28:07.430 --> 01:28:14.800
room. Where you keep the computer. Don't move. Part of you says the part that feels the sensation

01:28:14.850 --> 01:28:22.100
of cold water on your skin again. But there is another part of you now. And it's not cold with fear.

01:28:22.150 --> 01:28:27.960
It is hot. Hot with anger. Because you know now

01:28:28.010 --> 01:28:31.490
that someone is in your house. A burglar.

01:28:31.990 --> 01:28:39.850
Someone is here to steal your things. Don't move. Part of you says again. But you do.

01:28:41.150 --> 01:28:48.460
Quietly you move the blankets from your body and you take the dressing gown from the end of your bed and you put it on. You know you should

01:28:48.510 --> 01:28:51.060
call the police but you need to do something more.

01:28:51.110 --> 01:28:57.670
Something quick. At the side of your bed there is a table. And on the table there is a small statue.

01:28:58.790 --> 01:29:06.740
It's a statue of a woman. And it's your favorite statue. It's also a very heavy statue. You pick

01:29:06.790 --> 01:29:09.910
it up. Hold it like a club and swing it.

01:29:10.570 --> 01:29:14.110
Yes. It's heavy enough. One hit to the head.

01:29:14.890 --> 01:29:22.760
But can you do it? Can you hit someone? Can you hit a burglar? Then you hear another noise from

01:29:22.810 --> 01:29:27.650
the spare room. My computer you think. My new computer.

01:29:28.630 --> 01:29:36.360
And then you know that yes you can do it if you need to. If you have to. If there is someone in the next room trying to steal

01:29:36.410 --> 01:29:41.670
your new computer. Someone who thinks they can enter your house in the middle of the night.

01:29:42.670 --> 01:29:48.150
What if there are two people. Two burglars. Or three.

01:29:49.250 --> 01:29:52.530
Don't move. The scared part of you says.

01:29:53.490 --> 01:30:01.060
But you must. You walk silently to the door of your room. You quietly open it just a little

01:30:01.110 --> 01:30:08.170
so you can see the dark landing. Now you can hear the movement more easily. There are no voices.

01:30:08.830 --> 01:30:14.960
No reason for you to think that there is more than one burglar. You open the door a little more

01:30:15.010 --> 01:30:22.220
and step out onto the landing. It's colder here but you do not feel it. Your heart is beating so

01:30:22.270 --> 01:30:29.530
fast that you do not feel anything but the strange mixture of fear and anger. You move quietly.

01:30:30.610 --> 01:30:36.380
You're back to the wall. The light switch is not near your door but halfway to the stairs.

01:30:36.430 --> 01:30:42.750
The door to the spare room is half open. And you can hear the sound of the wind outside.

01:30:43.970 --> 01:30:50.410
You know now that the window to the spare room is open. And you know this is how the burglar entered.

01:30:51.350 --> 01:30:56.940
You take one more step to the door. Your hand is nearly on the light switch when you hear

01:30:56.990 --> 01:31:04.800
another noise from the room. Then the door begins to open slowly and quietly. You want to turn back

01:31:04.850 --> 01:31:09.550
and run to your room and hide under the blanket and be completely silent and not move.

01:31:10.610 --> 01:31:18.460
But this is your house. And you know you must stay. Then standing right in front of you in the dark

01:31:18.510 --> 01:31:25.760
you see the burglar. And he looks back at you and shouts. You turn on the lights and swing the

01:31:25.810 --> 01:31:31.330
heavy statue in his direction. But you cannot see him now because the light is so bright.

01:31:31.990 --> 01:31:37.630
You hear your new computer fall to the floor and break and the burglar shouts again.

01:31:38.630 --> 01:31:44.550
And then you can see his face. And you realize that he looks more afraid than you do.

01:31:45.330 --> 01:31:53.010
And he is stepping quickly backwards to the stairs. He is young. Just a kid. Just a stupid kid.

01:31:54.010 --> 01:32:00.440
Then you see Sophie in her normal place at the top of the stairs. And you see how near the

01:32:00.490 --> 01:32:07.950
burglaries. You only have time to shout two words. Don't move. But it is too late.

01:32:08.810 --> 01:32:16.330
And he does move. And then he falls. God is in the detail.

01:32:17.970 --> 01:32:23.870
The back streets of Dublin are quiet and the sun is slowly setting on the old city.

01:32:24.830 --> 01:32:31.630
Barry Brennan stands in the doorway of an empty shop and waits. He smokes his cigarettes continuously

01:32:32.170 --> 01:32:37.990
and enjoys the taste of each one. He enjoys another sweeter taste too.

01:32:39.130 --> 01:32:42.690
The taste of freedom. But he has to be careful.

01:32:43.490 --> 01:32:46.230
He knows that. The garden are everywhere.

01:32:47.230 --> 01:32:52.630
And they are looking for him. And if he wants to keep his freedom he has to be clever.

01:32:53.430 --> 01:32:59.790
He has to be patient. And so he watches the street. And the small red brick houses.

01:33:00.790 --> 01:33:08.790
Most importantly though he watches the church. His own parish church. The church of his childhood.

01:33:09.730 --> 01:33:13.980
St. Anthony's. How long ago was his last visit to

01:33:14.030 --> 01:33:18.070
this cold stone building? More than 20 years?

01:33:18.910 --> 01:33:26.160
Well that's not important at the moment. No. The only thing that's important is something that the

01:33:26.210 --> 01:33:31.920
church can offer him right now. The one thing he needs if he wants to keep his freedom and get

01:33:31.970 --> 01:33:38.180
the suitcase that's buried in his brother's old garage. The suitcase that can help him escape

01:33:38.230 --> 01:33:44.790
the country and live the rest of his life on a tropical island in the Bahamas. Sanctuary.

01:33:45.590 --> 01:33:52.600
And so he waits. And the evening becomes darker. And he thinks that there are now only one or two

01:33:52.650 --> 01:33:59.980
people left in the small old church. He decides that it's time. He pulls the collar of his stolen

01:34:00.030 --> 01:34:06.740
coat up to cover his face. And he checks that no one can see the grey prison uniform that he's

01:34:06.790 --> 01:34:14.300
still wearing beneath it. He finishes his last cigarette and stops to listen. In the distance

01:34:14.350 --> 01:34:20.230
he can hear the siren of a gardy car in the city centre. But he's sure it's not for him.

01:34:21.050 --> 01:34:27.240
So he crosses the road slowly and casually. Walks up the stone steps of the church and looks

01:34:27.290 --> 01:34:34.230
around once more before he pushes open the wooden doors and goes inside. St. Anthony's.

01:34:34.930 --> 01:34:39.340
It smells no different he thinks as he enters. And

01:34:39.390 --> 01:34:43.360
it looks identical too. The same cold grey

01:34:43.410 --> 01:34:51.130
walls. The same uncomfortable heavy pews. And the same weak candles to light up the dark room.

01:34:52.210 --> 01:34:58.770
He was right. There are a few people inside. Two women sitting together on the front pew.

01:34:59.310 --> 01:35:06.100
Their eyes closed and heads down. And an old man who looks at Barry for a moment before returning

01:35:06.150 --> 01:35:09.360
to his prayers. But not one of them is interested

01:35:09.410 --> 01:35:14.500
in him. Good. Barry thinks. The place is practically

01:35:14.550 --> 01:35:21.240
empty. He looks to the right wall and sees what he's looking for. A large wooden box with two

01:35:21.290 --> 01:35:29.200
doors. Quietly he walks past the pews and stops near the box. He pauses for a moment to try and

01:35:29.250 --> 01:35:35.770
remember what he has to do next. But then the door to the church opens again and three women enter.

01:35:36.490 --> 01:35:41.930
As he does not want them to see his face he pulls open the left door to the box and enters.

01:35:43.410 --> 01:35:47.290
A confessional. As soon as he's inside he hates it.

01:35:47.990 --> 01:35:51.220
It's small, so small and it reminds him of his

01:35:51.270 --> 01:35:57.440
prison cell back in the joy. Father. He says. But

01:35:57.490 --> 01:36:06.080
there is no reply and he can see no movement

01:36:06.130 --> 01:36:11.930
self comfortable on the small chair. But after a few minutes he realizes how tired he is.

01:36:12.470 --> 01:36:17.520
And he thinks that maybe he can close his eyes for a moment and even rest his head against the

01:36:17.570 --> 01:36:23.250
grill. But he mustn't sleep. He tells himself. He mustn't.

01:36:24.070 --> 01:36:31.180
But he does. And he dreams of the suitcase buried under his brother's old garage. And he dreams

01:36:31.230 --> 01:36:36.370
of the money inside and the paradise in the Bahamas. Hello?

01:36:37.350 --> 01:36:42.290
Says a voice. And Barry wakes up and looks around in confusion and fear.

01:36:43.350 --> 01:36:48.810
Is there someone there? Says the voice. Barry remembers where he is.

01:36:49.470 --> 01:36:56.110
Yes, I'm here. He says. And for a moment there is silence before he remembers what to say.

01:36:56.710 --> 01:37:03.780
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned. And again there is a long silence. Barry tries to see

01:37:03.830 --> 01:37:09.830
the face through the grill. But there is only a dark shape. Finally, the voice replies.

01:37:11.270 --> 01:37:16.480
I see. Go on. First, Father, I need to know

01:37:16.530 --> 01:37:23.550
something. I need to know that anything I say here is not to be repeated. That's how this works, isn't it?

01:37:24.670 --> 01:37:31.240
That is true. My son. The voice says. And Barry

01:37:31.290 --> 01:37:33.790
thinks that it's a calm and honest voice.

01:37:34.970 --> 01:37:40.890
Is that Father O'Connor? He asks, remembering his old priest. O'Connor?

01:37:42.190 --> 01:37:46.980
No, son. My name is Keen. I believe Father O

01:37:47.030 --> 01:37:49.690
'Connor passed away nearly ten years ago.

01:37:50.610 --> 01:37:57.830
Barry nods in the dark space. But does it matter that you're not my priest? Can you still listen?

01:37:58.770 --> 01:38:05.530
Any man can listen, says the quiet voice. But can you promise not to tell anyone?

01:38:06.310 --> 01:38:10.930
I promise, my son. I will not tell a soul.

01:38:11.950 --> 01:38:14.750
And Barry nods again and feels ready to speak.

01:38:15.630 --> 01:38:20.290
Father, I need to ask for something from you. I need to ask for sanctuary.

01:38:21.370 --> 01:38:28.210
Sanctuary? Yes, Father. I need somewhere to hide for a few days, a week maybe. Can you do that?

01:38:28.790 --> 01:38:32.510
Can you help me hide here in the church? There is another silence.

01:38:33.890 --> 01:38:39.750
Maybe. But why, my son? I can only help you if you tell me why.

01:38:40.770 --> 01:38:47.090
God, you see, is in the detail. If you want to confess and receive God's forgiveness,

01:38:47.990 --> 01:38:54.010
you must tell me everything. Okay, Father. But remember, you promise not to tell a soul?

01:38:55.210 --> 01:39:01.520
I do. Barry nods. My name is Barry Brennan. Do

01:39:01.570 --> 01:39:03.840
you know the name? Another pause.

01:39:03.890 --> 01:39:09.220
I do, my son. It's on every radio and every

01:39:09.270 --> 01:39:12.880
television. You are the prisoner, the one that

01:39:12.930 --> 01:39:17.420
escaped from Mount Joy Prison. My son, you should let

01:39:17.470 --> 01:39:21.010
me take you to the guards. No, Barry shouts.

01:39:21.910 --> 01:39:26.800
You promised, Father. No one can know. Then you must tell me everything.

01:39:26.850 --> 01:39:33.260
Everything. There's not much to tell. I escaped from the guardie van that was taking me to

01:39:33.310 --> 01:39:37.870
hospital. I told him I had a pain in my side and the doctors thought it was my appendix.

01:39:38.790 --> 01:39:43.590
When I was in the van, it stopped and I hit one of the guards, took his keys and ran.

01:39:44.630 --> 01:39:50.170
That was two days ago. Well, God will judge you for this, my son.

01:39:51.430 --> 01:39:53.930
But tell me, why did you stay in the city?

01:39:54.550 --> 01:39:56.030
Why not run for the countryside?

01:39:56.850 --> 01:40:00.510
Or hop on a ferry out of here? Well, Father,

01:40:00.790 --> 01:40:05.210
there's something I need to get before I can go.

01:40:05.990 --> 01:40:12.170
And that's why I'm here. I need to wait for a few days until they think I'm miles from the city.

01:40:13.170 --> 01:40:16.330
Then I can get this thing and I can go.

01:40:17.130 --> 01:40:25.000
And I promise, Father, after this, I will never commit another crime again. I will lead a quiet and honest

01:40:25.050 --> 01:40:29.000
life. And Barry imagines the beaches of the

01:40:29.050 --> 01:40:34.880
Bahamas. I see, says the voice, I want to help

01:40:34.930 --> 01:40:37.710
you. And if you confess all your sins to me,

01:40:37.870 --> 01:40:45.450
then maybe I can. But, like I say, God is in the detail. What is this thing you need?

01:40:46.750 --> 01:40:54.010
Barry hesitates. He thinks about lying, but why? The priest promises not to tell a soul.

01:40:55.330 --> 01:41:02.310
It's a suitcase, Father, and inside there is half a million euros. And yes, it is stolen,

01:41:02.690 --> 01:41:08.120
but I promise, Father, no one needed now. I stole it more than eight years ago from a bank

01:41:08.170 --> 01:41:13.020
security van, a bank, Father, and they have insurance and all that, so they don't need it.

01:41:13.070 --> 01:41:19.250
No one needs it now, apart from me. And this time the silence is heavy and tense.

01:41:20.450 --> 01:41:25.520
And Barry can hear the voices of the women as they leave the church. He wonders if there

01:41:25.570 --> 01:41:29.150
are still any people there. But he thinks there are not.

01:41:31.010 --> 01:41:36.420
Well, says the quiet voice. I understand, my son. And

01:41:36.470 --> 01:41:37.450
I will help you.

01:41:38.150 --> 01:41:40.390
I can let you sleep here for a few days,

01:41:40.770 --> 01:41:42.050
and I can bring you some food.

01:41:42.850 --> 01:41:47.830
Oh, Father, thank you, Father. But, like I say, God is in the detail.

01:41:48.830 --> 01:41:56.180
Where exactly is this suitcase? What? Why? Why? Because I

01:41:56.230 --> 01:42:02.980
need to know that you aren't going to hurt anyone to get it. The voice says seriously. Barry

01:42:03.030 --> 01:42:05.560
loves. Oh, no, Father, I promise.

01:42:05.610 --> 01:42:11.690
It's in an old garage of my brother. On a bit of land next to the house road. I can go at night,

01:42:12.070 --> 01:42:15.370
dig up the concrete, and get it no problem. Well,

01:42:15.550 --> 01:42:18.010
I tell you what, you wait here.

01:42:18.830 --> 01:42:24.310
I'm going to close the church. Then maybe I can find you that bit of food and some other clothes.

01:42:25.330 --> 01:42:32.350
And for the first time in two days, Barry Brennan feels relief. He can really taste the freedom now.

01:42:33.130 --> 01:42:35.330
Ah, thank you, Father. Thank you.

01:42:35.930 --> 01:42:40.570
You know, I'm sure there's a bit of spare money for the church. 3,000 maybe.

01:42:41.110 --> 01:42:48.180
What do you think? But there is no answer. There is, however, a strange noise. Something moving

01:42:48.230 --> 01:42:55.310
against the door of the confessional. Something heavy. Something like one of the heavy pews.

01:42:55.950 --> 01:43:02.920
And Barry tries to push the door open. But he cannot. And before Barry Brennan begins to

01:43:02.970 --> 01:43:08.370
shout and scream to the empty church, he hears the soft voice one more time.

01:43:09.310 --> 01:43:12.670
Now, Son, I told you that any man can listen,

01:43:12.910 --> 01:43:16.180
didn't I? It says. And I also told you that

01:43:16.230 --> 01:43:20.680
God is in the detail. And an important detail, a very

01:43:20.730 --> 01:43:28.200
important detail. Is that you're in the wrong side of the confessional? I just stopped in to confess

01:43:28.250 --> 01:43:33.500
about stealing another bottle of whiskey from the shop. But you know, I think I can

01:43:33.550 --> 01:43:37.320
do that next time. Yes, I think I might have a lot more

01:43:37.370 --> 01:43:45.260
to confess tomorrow. No, you did say howth road. And then there is only the sound of Barry Brennan's

01:43:45.310 --> 01:43:52.870
shouts. And there is no one in the church to hear those. Well, apart from God, I suppose.

01:43:55.830 --> 01:44:02.000
Route 66. The car is big and old, but it's

01:44:02.050 --> 01:44:05.850
a classic. And it moves along the road like a shark,

01:44:06.930 --> 01:44:11.140
the evening sun reflecting off the red paint. The roof

01:44:11.190 --> 01:44:18.920
is down. And Dan enjoys the feeling of the wind in his hair. It calls him and helps him

01:44:18.970 --> 01:44:23.960
to stay awake. He's tired, very tired, and he still

01:44:24.010 --> 01:44:27.190
feels sick from the beer and whiskey of last night.

01:44:28.090 --> 01:44:32.300
Soon he can stop, find a quiet area off the road

01:44:32.350 --> 01:44:36.170
and push back the chair and sleep. But not yet.

01:44:37.170 --> 01:44:44.980
The Mojave desert is still all around him with its flat orange sands and dead bushes. The plan was

01:44:45.030 --> 01:44:51.320
to get to Flagstaff, but it's too many miles away. So instead he just wants to get as far

01:44:51.370 --> 01:44:54.720
from the town called Needles as he can before the night

01:44:54.770 --> 01:45:02.040
comes. Needles. He hates that town. And that girl. He

01:45:02.090 --> 01:45:04.230
looks at his right hand on the steering wheel.

01:45:04.810 --> 01:45:09.080
The skin is raw, and he tries to remember exactly

01:45:09.130 --> 01:45:13.500
what happened last night, but he cannot. And maybe

01:45:13.550 --> 01:45:19.280
he should not. Stupid girl. But anyway, why think about

01:45:19.330 --> 01:45:27.290
it? Just drive, get a few more miles from the town and from her. He hits the button for the radio,

01:45:27.950 --> 01:45:30.780
and an old song fills the silence of the desert.

01:45:30.830 --> 01:45:35.880
A good song, he thinks. A real song. All guitars

01:45:35.930 --> 01:45:39.510
and rock and roll. He feels a little better.

01:45:40.410 --> 01:45:46.100
Maybe it's time for the first drink of the day after all, and he takes the small flask from the

01:45:46.150 --> 01:45:50.030
pocket of his denim jacket, removes the lid, and swallows.

01:45:50.730 --> 01:45:53.250
For a second, the taste is foul.

01:45:54.030 --> 01:46:01.320
A horrible reminder of last night, of the girl and of the hotel room. But then the hot whiskey

01:46:01.370 --> 01:46:07.750
starts to fill him, and he smiles, puts his head back and laughs loudly into the desert.

01:46:08.490 --> 01:46:15.810
Route 66! He shouts, and he turns the volume of the radio up. He can forget about last night,

01:46:16.210 --> 01:46:22.860
about the girl. His shirt has a little blood on it, and his hand is sore. But he can get a new

01:46:22.910 --> 01:46:29.460
shirt, and the whiskey will help the pain. No, this is his holiday, his escape from all those

01:46:29.510 --> 01:46:34.090
idiots back in California. Forget needles and forget the girl.

01:46:34.930 --> 01:46:37.090
Just relax and enjoy it.

01:46:37.790 --> 01:46:43.680
She was not the first girl, and he knows she will not be the last. Maybe there's one waiting for

01:46:43.730 --> 01:46:50.560
him in Flagstaff, and this one, he hopes, will have a little more respect for him and won't laugh at

01:46:50.610 --> 01:46:57.970
him. So he drives, and for a while he feels better. The sun is lower in the sky now.

01:46:58.510 --> 01:47:03.710
It's a little less hot, and the radio keeps him company with old songs about love and women,

01:47:04.270 --> 01:47:09.080
rock and roll and country music, real music. But then

01:47:09.130 --> 01:47:12.330
the songs stop, and the news report starts.

01:47:13.230 --> 01:47:17.310
There's something about needles, something about trouble on Route 66,

01:47:18.070 --> 01:47:25.910
and he tries to listen, but the signal is weak. A young, dead, the police are looking, a man, probably alone,

01:47:26.190 --> 01:47:31.190
and thought to be extremely dangerous, any information call, and then the signal fails,

01:47:31.850 --> 01:47:35.750
and there is only the sound of the old classic engine and the silence of the desert.

01:47:37.470 --> 01:47:43.190
Dead? No, no, it can't be her. He looks at his hand again and tries to make a fist,

01:47:43.810 --> 01:47:50.760
but it hurts. And he thinks of the white shirt in the trunk of the car, the white shirt with blood

01:47:50.810 --> 01:47:54.960
on it. What did he do last night? He tries

01:47:55.010 --> 01:47:58.890
to remember, he tries to remember exactly what happened.

01:47:59.630 --> 01:48:05.690
He remembers the bar clearly, he remembers buying her a drink, and then another, and another.

01:48:06.350 --> 01:48:09.230
He remembers that afterwards they were in the hotel room,

01:48:09.510 --> 01:48:15.580
kissing. But then something happened, and she laughed. She laughed at him, but he cannot

01:48:15.630 --> 01:48:18.170
remember why. It was something stupid,

01:48:18.710 --> 01:48:24.290
something he said or did. And then? But the rest is impossible to remember.

01:48:25.190 --> 01:48:31.210
He woke up late in the hotel room and was alone. He saw the blood, but there was only a little,

01:48:31.530 --> 01:48:39.470
and his hand hurt. And he knew. Yeah, he could not remember, but he knew.

01:48:40.290 --> 01:48:44.090
And he thinks of all the times he watched his father hit his mother.

01:48:44.870 --> 01:48:48.810
Jesus! He says, and he does not feel tired anymore.

01:48:49.770 --> 01:48:54.470
He puts his foot on the accelerator, and the car starts to speed along the old road.

01:48:55.430 --> 01:48:58.050
Forget sleeping. Forget Flagstaff.

01:48:58.850 --> 01:49:03.160
He needs to find another route and a motel to stay in for a few days.

01:49:03.210 --> 01:49:09.750
A cheap motel, where they only want a name and some cash. The sun is almost down now.

01:49:10.310 --> 01:49:17.840
The sky a deep orange and red. And the road is empty. Or it is until he speeds past the large

01:49:17.890 --> 01:49:22.900
billboard. Then suddenly the road is not empty. There is

01:49:22.950 --> 01:49:30.510
a car behind him. Its red and blue lights fill the evening. And the sound of the siren fills his ears.

01:49:31.490 --> 01:49:35.210
No! He groans as he sees it in the mirror.

01:49:36.110 --> 01:49:38.490
And he thinks for a moment about not stopping.

01:49:39.370 --> 01:49:45.410
But there is nowhere for him to go. Jesus, just stay cool down, just stay cool.

01:49:46.050 --> 01:49:51.870
He tries to tell himself as the red car slows down. So he stops on the edge of the desert,

01:49:52.670 --> 01:49:57.070
hides the flask of whiskey under the seat and lights a cigarette to cover the smell.

01:49:57.730 --> 01:50:03.670
God, he wishes he could remember last night. Not just the girl's expression when he hit her.

01:50:04.370 --> 01:50:08.870
But everything. In the mirror he sees the cop car park behind him,

01:50:09.270 --> 01:50:16.180
and he watches a young tall cop get out. Just one. Good. If things get serious then maybe he

01:50:16.230 --> 01:50:21.310
can manage if there's just one. He watches the cop walk slowly to the car.

01:50:22.310 --> 01:50:28.370
The guy's eyes are covered by silver sunglasses. And there's something strange about his uniform.

01:50:29.330 --> 01:50:35.390
Dan thinks. But before he can decide what, the cop is at the door and Dan drops his cigarette.

01:50:36.130 --> 01:50:41.660
Evening officer, he says, and tries to look sorry. I know I was going a little too fast,

01:50:41.710 --> 01:50:49.060
I'm sorry. The cop looks at him and nods, but says nothing. Dan wants to see the eyes behind

01:50:49.110 --> 01:50:56.080
the glasses, but cannot. I suppose it's this road. The desert makes it easy to forget to

01:50:56.130 --> 01:51:02.990
watch your speed. He says, and he thinks he sees the cop look at his hand. Jesus. Is this it?

01:51:03.750 --> 01:51:07.810
Did he really do something to that girl? More than hit her once.

01:51:08.530 --> 01:51:12.050
Did he do what they said on the radio? Was she dead?

01:51:13.590 --> 01:51:18.290
Yep, the cop says. These roads are funny like that.

01:51:19.090 --> 01:51:21.290
Lots of people have problems out here.

01:51:22.210 --> 01:51:28.620
This is a nice car. A classic. Yours? And his

01:51:28.670 --> 01:51:35.950
voice is slow. Yes sir, I have my license here in the registration. The cop nods, but does not take them.

01:51:36.770 --> 01:51:38.930
I need to ask you to get out of the car.

01:51:39.790 --> 01:51:45.890
Dan tries to smile. Is there a problem officer? And he looks at the gun on the cop's belt.

01:51:46.610 --> 01:51:48.590
If he can get the gun, then he can escape.

01:51:49.550 --> 01:51:53.510
Forget California, go north to Canada or Alaska.

01:51:54.530 --> 01:51:59.530
But he waits. Maybe this is something else, a routine check, a formality.

01:52:00.230 --> 01:52:07.290
Be cool, Dan. He tells himself. Just get out of the car, please. The cop repeats.

01:52:08.370 --> 01:52:15.150
Dan nods and gets out. And he thinks again that there is something strange about the cop's uniform.

01:52:16.330 --> 01:52:19.790
It's small. Too small for a tall man like him.

01:52:20.970 --> 01:52:23.230
I need to see in the trunk of the car,

01:52:23.490 --> 01:52:23.670
sir.

01:52:24.770 --> 01:52:32.670
Jesus. The shirt. The shirt is in the trunk. Is that what he wants? The trunk? Can I ask why?

01:52:33.550 --> 01:52:39.820
Just routine. Just routine. Maybe it is. And the shirt

01:52:39.870 --> 01:52:42.610
is in his bag at the bottom where it cannot be seen.

01:52:43.430 --> 01:52:50.530
Okay? He walks around to the trunk. The cop behind him. And he pushes the button.

01:52:51.150 --> 01:52:57.760
For a moment, he does nothing. He remembers the baseball bat he keeps in there. If the cop asks

01:52:57.810 --> 01:53:03.370
to look in his bag, he can reach in, grab the bat and take a swing. Maybe it can work.

01:53:04.990 --> 01:53:10.900
Sir? The cop says. Sure. And Dan starts to lift

01:53:10.950 --> 01:53:17.530
the lid of the trunk. But in the reflection of the red paint, he sees the cop raise his hand.

01:53:18.450 --> 01:53:21.370
And in the soft evening light, he sees the knife.

01:53:22.330 --> 01:53:28.510
And for a moment, he does not understand. But then he moves. He is almost quick enough.

01:53:29.350 --> 01:53:35.100
But the knife still cuts him and he screams and falls to the floor. The cop falls too and there

01:53:35.150 --> 01:53:42.080
is a moment of confusion. But then Dan pushes himself up onto his feet and runs. He sees the

01:53:42.130 --> 01:53:47.190
cop car and runs to it and pulls open the door. That's when he sees the bodies.

01:53:48.190 --> 01:53:53.170
One is clearly a police woman. But the other, a short man, has no uniform.

01:53:54.210 --> 01:53:58.710
Jesus! He screams and he remembers the news story on the radio.

01:53:59.750 --> 01:54:06.040
A young, dead, the police are looking a man, probably alone and thought to be extremely dangerous.

01:54:06.090 --> 01:54:11.870
Any information, call. And he starts to run again, not on the road now, but into the desert.

01:54:12.710 --> 01:54:17.290
He wants to turn to look back to see if the man is following him. But he does not dare.

01:54:18.050 --> 01:54:23.450
He thinks that if he keeps running, he can escape and find help. Find the real police.

01:54:24.390 --> 01:54:30.740
But the sun is gone now and darkness is all around him. Now everything from last night

01:54:30.790 --> 01:54:34.590
is coming back to him. He remembers hitting the girl.

01:54:35.170 --> 01:54:43.020
He remembers her shouting at him and he remembers telling her to get out. And he remembers the last thing she said before she

01:54:43.070 --> 01:54:45.010
closed the door to the hotel room.

01:54:45.790 --> 01:54:51.730
I hope you die and rot in the desert, she shouted, blood still on her face.

01:54:52.530 --> 01:55:00.070
And he almost laughs in disbelief. But then he hears someone running behind him and he screams instead.

01:55:01.950 --> 01:55:02.690
The Fall

01:55:04.930 --> 01:55:10.010
Brandon tries not to think as he walks through the snow that reaches up to his waist.

01:55:11.210 --> 01:55:17.620
He is confused and weak. He tries not to remember the expression on Greg's face as the

01:55:17.670 --> 01:55:25.020
bullet hit him in the back. You said it was easy. Brandon calls out into the forest of

01:55:25.070 --> 01:55:29.230
silent trees that surround him, his voice full of emotion.

01:55:30.310 --> 01:55:32.010
You said no one could get hurt.

01:55:33.010 --> 01:55:40.030
But someone did get hurt. And now Brandon is alone and he feels tired and lost.

01:55:41.090 --> 01:55:45.010
And he knows that the man in the dark winter coat and black hat is near.

01:55:45.890 --> 01:55:48.650
And he thinks that the next bullet is for him.

01:55:49.370 --> 01:55:51.890
He tries to move faster through the snow,

01:55:52.390 --> 01:55:59.550
but it's so deep. And it is so cold. His clothes are wet and his skin frozen.

01:56:00.630 --> 01:56:02.690
He wants to put his hand in his coat pocket,

01:56:03.190 --> 01:56:05.170
but he has to keep the grey pistol ready.

01:56:06.130 --> 01:56:12.000
He remembers the way that Greg fell. One moment they were running, the next there was the sound

01:56:12.050 --> 01:56:18.500
of the gun and a scream. Then Greg was on the floor. Brandon wanted to stop and help him.

01:56:18.550 --> 01:56:23.870
But Greg told him to run, to escape and to not say anything to anyone.

01:56:24.990 --> 01:56:30.710
And then Brandon heard another gunshot, so he ran and left his brother in the snow to die alone.

01:56:31.650 --> 01:56:38.180
You said it was easy. He shouts again and recalls the first time his brother had told him about

01:56:38.230 --> 01:56:44.830
the plan. Listen to me. His brother said at a dark table in the corner at the lake Louise Inn.

01:56:45.590 --> 01:56:50.590
It's easy, Bran. I know exactly what to do. Brandon put his beer down.

01:56:51.330 --> 01:56:54.050
But a kid, Greg. I don't want to hurt a kid.

01:56:55.470 --> 01:56:59.990
What? His brother said, his eyes full of anger. You think I do?

01:57:00.750 --> 01:57:04.790
Idiot, we won't hurt anyone. We just take the kid in the truck.

01:57:05.650 --> 01:57:10.740
Then we phone the hotel. We tell them to tell the parents they can have the kid when they leave

01:57:10.790 --> 01:57:14.290
us the money. It's easy. No one can get hurt.

01:57:15.010 --> 01:57:16.590
But Brandon was not so sure.

01:57:17.330 --> 01:57:23.360
He knew his brother was a criminal. He knew that he brewed illegal beer to sell and that he drove

01:57:23.410 --> 01:57:30.530
drugs across Alberta and British Columbia. But Brandon did not do that. He did not do any of that.

01:57:31.310 --> 01:57:38.450
He worked in the lumberyard. And he tried to do things right. He tried to be better than that.

01:57:39.150 --> 01:57:39.670
But no.

01:57:40.990 --> 01:57:45.110
Brandon, his brother, said, I know you need the money.

01:57:45.650 --> 01:57:51.520
You don't have a job. Your truck is in pieces. You can't find work if you can't drive. What

01:57:51.570 --> 01:57:54.330
does Sally say? Brandon shook his head.

01:57:55.330 --> 01:58:01.030
Sally left me last month. He said, and he called the waiter for another beer.

01:58:01.770 --> 01:58:04.780
Tell me again. Tell me everything again.

01:58:04.830 --> 01:58:10.750
Now alone in the forest, he thinks he can hear the sound of feet in the snow behind him.

01:58:11.410 --> 01:58:18.880
And he tries to move faster. There is another sound too. A sound that he recognises. A sound

01:58:18.930 --> 01:58:22.790
that's getting nearer. It's like constant thunder in the distance.

01:58:23.810 --> 01:58:31.150
And he pushes through the snow and he thinks that maybe, if he can get to the sound, he can flee into the mountains and never come back.

01:58:31.950 --> 01:58:36.830
Look, every year, Greg said, hundreds of tourists come here,

01:58:36.950 --> 01:58:38.770
right? Most of them are rich.

01:58:39.590 --> 01:58:45.030
And some of them are very rich. They want to ski in the spring, hike in the summer,

01:58:45.250 --> 01:58:51.390
and spend their money in the hotel bar in the spa. Some of these people are millionaires, Brandon.

01:58:52.890 --> 01:58:58.180
Millionaires? Think about that. Greg drank some more of his beer and looked around the bar.

01:58:58.230 --> 01:59:03.910
Look at them. Their clothes are better than ours. Their cars and trucks are better.

01:59:04.510 --> 01:59:08.910
Everything they have is better. And listen, I don't hate them for that. I don't.

01:59:09.490 --> 01:59:15.250
I just know that if we ask them for a small amount, then they can pay. I mean, 20,000.

01:59:15.550 --> 01:59:15.810
No more.

01:59:16.670 --> 01:59:21.530
If you ask for more, you have to wait. And then there's the risk that they contact the police.

01:59:22.290 --> 01:59:24.390
They can get 20,000 from the bank at bank.

01:59:25.030 --> 01:59:27.230
We tell them they only have six hours.

01:59:27.910 --> 01:59:29.990
We tell them to leave the money where we say,

01:59:30.410 --> 01:59:33.290
or, or what, Greg? Or what?

01:59:34.150 --> 01:59:37.290
On that first day, Brandon said no to the plan.

01:59:38.090 --> 01:59:39.930
On the second day, he said no again.

01:59:40.990 --> 01:59:43.550
On the third day, his truck stopped on the highway.

01:59:44.430 --> 01:59:46.950
He had about 300 bucks left in the world.

01:59:48.030 --> 01:59:54.420
Sally was gone. Their cabin by the lake was cold and empty. There were no jobs for him

01:59:54.470 --> 01:59:58.370
anywhere. So on the fourth day, he said yes.

01:59:59.690 --> 02:00:05.850
The sound is louder now. Yes, it's a sound he knows. A sound he knows well.

02:00:06.750 --> 02:00:13.090
He can see nothing because of the trees, but he knows he is very near. He feels warmer now,

02:00:13.370 --> 02:00:20.330
and this scares him. In Canada, in the mountains, when you're cold, you're okay. But when you're warm,

02:00:21.090 --> 02:00:27.860
you were in trouble. What time is it now? He does not know. He only knows that it was

02:00:27.910 --> 02:00:31.710
three in the afternoon when they first heard the sound of the police sirens.

02:00:32.790 --> 02:00:35.050
We have to go. Brandon shouted.

02:00:36.110 --> 02:00:42.140
How? How can they know we're here? Said Greg. Brandon pulled him from the old deserted cabin

02:00:42.190 --> 02:00:46.940
and they ran for Greg's truck. The sound of the sirens was still in the distance,

02:00:46.990 --> 02:00:52.800
and Brandon thought that maybe they were still okay and that maybe they could leave. Maybe they

02:00:52.850 --> 02:00:59.360
could escape together. But then there was that gunshot, and the man in the dark winter coat and

02:00:59.410 --> 02:01:06.560
black hat who appeared from behind the trees. So Brandon ran, and he got to the truck and managed

02:01:06.610 --> 02:01:14.050
to drive to the road, where for a few minutes he thought he was free. But then another truck appeared on the road behind him.

02:01:15.270 --> 02:01:19.420
But which kid? Brandon asked, as they planned everything in

02:01:19.470 --> 02:01:20.130
Greg's cabin.

02:01:21.030 --> 02:01:26.260
There is a family who comes here maybe three or four times a year. The wife is a pretty little

02:01:26.310 --> 02:01:32.390
thing with red hair. I think she has family here, or maybe she comes from the area. I don't know.

02:01:33.070 --> 02:01:38.120
The husband is rich, very rich, and American. The kid

02:01:38.170 --> 02:01:45.070
looks about eleven, and they let him walk into the town from the hotel sometimes. So we watch,

02:01:45.610 --> 02:01:47.810
and we wait. When he's alone,

02:01:48.250 --> 02:01:55.640
we take him. I phone the hotel. Six hours, that's it. That's enough time for them to get the money

02:01:55.690 --> 02:02:02.620
from Banff. But not enough to do anything else like phone the cops. It's easy. So that's what

02:02:02.670 --> 02:02:10.220
they did. They took the kid as he walked into town. There was no one on the snowy road. No one saw

02:02:10.270 --> 02:02:16.830
them. But they covered Greg's plates to be sure, and they wore masks. The kids screamed at first,

02:02:17.270 --> 02:02:22.720
but they gagged him and tied his hands. Greg made the call to the hotel, and they drove to the

02:02:22.770 --> 02:02:29.750
cabin in the Yoho National Park. Then they waited. Six hours to leave them the money in a safe place.

02:02:30.610 --> 02:02:37.590
No police or the kid dies. That's what Greg told the hotel. Even if that part of it was not true.

02:02:38.150 --> 02:02:45.350
So how did it all go wrong? There are fewer trees now, and the sound is louder, much louder,

02:02:46.010 --> 02:02:53.260
like the shouting of a giant. The tachychor waterfalls. Brandon watches the clear water tumble onto the

02:02:53.310 --> 02:02:57.060
rocks, through the snow and down to the frozen forest

02:02:57.110 --> 02:03:04.760
below. Why here? He doesn't know. He tried to escape from the truck that chased him, but he

02:03:04.810 --> 02:03:07.850
crashed on the highway. The next thing he knew,

02:03:07.950 --> 02:03:10.970
he was holding his shoulder and walking confused and weak.

02:03:12.050 --> 02:03:19.560
The falls. So beautiful. Just like when he and Greg were kids. He wants to sit down

02:03:19.610 --> 02:03:22.290
to look at them, and moves closer to the edge.

02:03:23.270 --> 02:03:28.940
But no, he cannot sit down. He's sure that the man with the gun is near. So instead,

02:03:28.990 --> 02:03:33.250
he stands on the edge. He feels warm and weak,

02:03:33.970 --> 02:03:35.030
and his shoulder hurts.

02:03:36.090 --> 02:03:41.550
And he can't remember where Greg is. He said it was easy. He says.

02:03:42.830 --> 02:03:48.710
But it wasn't. A voice replies. And Brandon turns and sees the dark coat.

02:03:49.350 --> 02:03:55.090
Only this time there is no black hat. Just long red hair. And a rifle.

02:03:57.930 --> 02:04:05.310
How? How did you find us? You only had a few hours to get the money. My husband went for the money,

02:04:05.570 --> 02:04:11.180
says the woman, rifling one hand's black hat in the other. But I went to the road where you

02:04:11.230 --> 02:04:14.470
took my son. I saw the tracks from your truck.

02:04:15.410 --> 02:04:22.420
And I told the police. But I made sure I found the cabin first. I know these mountains better than they

02:04:22.470 --> 02:04:24.350
do, and better than you.

02:04:25.170 --> 02:04:31.920
I'm sorry, Brandon says. I really am sorry. The boy

02:04:31.970 --> 02:04:34.710
is okay. He's in the back room of the cabin.

02:04:35.430 --> 02:04:39.820
I know, the woman says. Are you going to shoot

02:04:39.870 --> 02:04:44.360
me? He asks. There is only the sound of the

02:04:44.410 --> 02:04:51.520
waterfall for a moment. Then the woman turns and moves back into the trees. He feels his leg

02:04:51.570 --> 02:04:55.180
shake and falls down onto the red and white snow

02:04:55.230 --> 02:05:00.080
by the waterfall. I already did. She calls back

02:05:00.130 --> 02:05:04.440
to him as she slowly disappears from sight. The old

02:05:04.490 --> 02:05:14.340
school tie. The police car stops in the

02:05:14.390 --> 02:05:21.140
detective Smith is older. His hair a mixture of black and gray, his stomach large, and his

02:05:21.190 --> 02:05:28.720
expression usually unhappy. The other detective West is younger and thinner, but with an empty

02:05:28.770 --> 02:05:33.570
look in his eye. Look at this place, says Smith.

02:05:34.470 --> 02:05:36.230
In the distance, the church towers,

02:05:36.370 --> 02:05:40.730
cathedral and university buildings of the city of Oxford are all visible.

02:05:41.570 --> 02:05:48.430
Around the old but impressive school, there are green gardens and grounds that continue for miles.

02:05:49.910 --> 02:05:54.860
And at the windows of the building, there are the thirty faces of serious young men who watch

02:05:54.910 --> 02:05:59.380
them strangely. God, what kind of kid goes to a

02:05:59.430 --> 02:06:03.520
school like this? West nods, but does not say

02:06:03.570 --> 02:06:10.440
anything. After only two weeks of working with Smith, he already knows that it's best to just

02:06:10.490 --> 02:06:15.600
nod. Come on, Smith says, and they walk to the

02:06:15.650 --> 02:06:23.120
entrance. Inside, the school smells of wood and polish, and on the walls there are antique pictures of

02:06:23.170 --> 02:06:25.310
old teachers and respected students.

02:06:26.030 --> 02:06:31.200
Not like my school. Sir? I went to a real

02:06:31.250 --> 02:06:34.650
school. A comprehensive. You?

02:06:35.490 --> 02:06:40.370
A grammar school, sir. And Smith nods his head and says nothing.

02:06:41.270 --> 02:06:46.730
The secretary outside the headmaster's room tells them they can enter, but Smith stops.

02:06:47.650 --> 02:06:51.610
Listen, West, I do the talking. You make the notes.

02:06:52.790 --> 02:06:54.720
The best thing a good detective can do

02:06:54.770 --> 02:07:01.080
is listen and observe. Write that down. Yes, sir? The

02:07:01.130 --> 02:07:03.750
headmaster is a man about the same age as Smith.

02:07:04.650 --> 02:07:10.790
But there the comparison ends. Smith looks tired, old, and unhappy.

02:07:12.030 --> 02:07:16.770
The headmaster, Mr. Bowen, does not exactly look happy, but he looks content.

02:07:17.810 --> 02:07:23.530
Content with his job, his life, his expensive house, and his two expensive cars.

02:07:24.590 --> 02:07:32.400
Smith hates him instantly. The secretary, a young and pretty blonde, enters the room and sits by the

02:07:32.450 --> 02:07:37.250
table. The headmaster tells them to sit in the same voice he uses for his students.

02:07:38.710 --> 02:07:42.490
Miss White here is to take notes and give you some more details about Mr. Fletcher.

02:07:43.550 --> 02:07:50.020
Mr. Fletcher asks Smith. Yes, Bowen stands by the large

02:07:50.070 --> 02:07:51.710
windows and does not look at them.

02:07:52.590 --> 02:07:58.280
He's why you're here, don't you know that? No, the chief inspector told us to come,

02:07:58.330 --> 02:08:04.050
but he didn't say why. Ah, yes, James. We play golf together. Good man.

02:08:04.990 --> 02:08:09.790
Well, the problem is that we can't find one of our members of staff, Mr. Fletcher.

02:08:11.090 --> 02:08:15.840
A missing person. I see. When did you last see

02:08:15.890 --> 02:08:20.830
him? Miss White? His last class was yesterday at four.

02:08:21.650 --> 02:08:26.800
He was teaching the upper sixth form boys, geometry. They say he finished class at five and left as

02:08:26.850 --> 02:08:32.740
normal. I see. Then he went home? Mr. Fletcher lives

02:08:32.790 --> 02:08:38.300
on the grounds of the school several of the older teachers do. He lives alone on the second floor

02:08:38.350 --> 02:08:44.340
in the east wing. Two other teachers live there, but neither saw him. Smith is about to speak

02:08:44.390 --> 02:08:46.690
when a loud hammering sound starts,

02:08:47.170 --> 02:08:51.430
and Bowen shakes his head. Sorry about that building work.

02:08:51.990 --> 02:08:58.440
Several areas in the school are being renovated. Smith speaks up. And did he leave the

02:08:58.490 --> 02:09:01.250
school last night? We don't know.

02:09:01.410 --> 02:09:03.910
He normally doesn't. He takes his meals in the room,

02:09:04.110 --> 02:09:05.850
but last night he didn't call for anything.

02:09:06.650 --> 02:09:13.610
Is that strange? Not uncommon. Sometimes he prefers to have a sherry under cigar and nothing more,

02:09:14.130 --> 02:09:19.290
but when he didn't come to breakfast this morning, we began to worry. Smith looks at the clock.

02:09:20.030 --> 02:09:25.950
Mr. Bowen, normally we need to wait 48 hours until we can start a missing person investigation.

02:09:26.990 --> 02:09:33.200
Bowen turns from the window and gives Smith an annoyed look. Your chief inspector said you could

02:09:33.250 --> 02:09:37.730
help. Was he wrong? Smith sighs and shakes his head.

02:09:38.590 --> 02:09:41.240
Does Mr. Fletcher have any good friends here?

02:09:41.290 --> 02:09:47.200
Or more importantly, enemies. He is a solitary man. But

02:09:47.250 --> 02:09:49.960
respected. He is the oldest teacher at

02:09:50.010 --> 02:09:54.570
Barnaby's, practically part of the building. Bowen stops and thinks.

02:09:55.530 --> 02:10:02.610
He doesn't like our new music teacher, Mr. Cliff. Didn't they have some sort of fight recently, Miss White? I don't think so.

02:10:03.370 --> 02:10:08.830
She says quietly. And what about the students? Smith asks them both.

02:10:09.710 --> 02:10:16.280
The students replies Bowen in an insulted tone. Detective, the

02:10:16.330 --> 02:10:22.020
young men at this school are very clever and come from very important families. In a few years

02:10:22.070 --> 02:10:24.510
they will be politicians, judges or doctors.

02:10:25.430 --> 02:10:29.510
They are not criminals. Smith breathes out with a puff.

02:10:30.510 --> 02:10:33.780
No. Well, I suppose we could have a look

02:10:33.830 --> 02:10:38.900
around the school. Good, says Bowen. Show them out, Miss

02:10:38.950 --> 02:10:46.840
White. And the headmaster does not look at them again. Jesus, says Smith in the corridor. Who

02:10:46.890 --> 02:10:53.940
does he think he is? Just because he plays golf with the chief inspector? Waste of bloody time this

02:10:53.990 --> 02:11:02.820
is West. Did you hear him? This

02:11:02.870 --> 02:11:09.660
pub in the city. And West nods again. They walk along the corridors for five minutes

02:11:09.710 --> 02:11:17.060
and reach an internal courtyard. Outside they can see the renovation work. There's scaffolding on

02:11:17.110 --> 02:11:21.980
the face of the old building, a new wall on the side of it and three or four workmen are sitting

02:11:22.030 --> 02:11:26.760
on a bench eating lunch. They cross the courtyard. And

02:11:26.810 --> 02:11:34.180
Smith stops to pick up a school tie on the floor by the new wall. Politicians and doctors, they can't

02:11:34.230 --> 02:11:35.110
even dress themselves.

02:11:36.610 --> 02:11:40.170
And Smith puts the tie in his pocket. Sir, look.

02:11:40.890 --> 02:11:43.390
West says and points to the top of a tower.

02:11:44.050 --> 02:11:49.590
What? There are two people watching us, sir. And? Well,

02:11:49.770 --> 02:11:59.500
just observing, sir. Maybe there's a

02:11:59.550 --> 02:12:04.170
one we go. We can also check one or two of the local pubs on the way back to the city.

02:12:05.010 --> 02:12:10.300
The door to the tower is in another corridor. And when they open it, they hear footsteps on the

02:12:10.350 --> 02:12:16.080
stairs. Who's that? Smith shouts. The footsteps stop and Miss

02:12:16.130 --> 02:12:18.610
White steps into view. You?

02:12:19.610 --> 02:12:25.690
Were you watching us from the roof? No, well, not really. The secretary says quietly.

02:12:26.590 --> 02:12:32.900
Who else is with you? Mr. Fletcher. What? No. Be

02:12:32.950 --> 02:12:35.150
honest now or there might be trouble for you.

02:12:36.230 --> 02:12:42.570
It's my fault. A voice says and a young man steps forward. Miss White came to speak to me.

02:12:43.490 --> 02:12:47.010
Oh, really? And who are you? I'm the music teacher,

02:12:47.290 --> 02:12:47.710
Mr. Cliff.

02:12:48.810 --> 02:12:53.530
Ah, well, isn't that interesting? No, it's not what you think.

02:12:54.210 --> 02:12:57.810
I came here to tell Mr. Cliff what the headmaster said about the argument.

02:12:58.830 --> 02:13:04.320
Go on. Look, says Cliff. It was nothing, really. Only

02:13:04.370 --> 02:13:06.130
Fletcher said something about Miss White,

02:13:06.470 --> 02:13:12.040
something no gentleman should say. I told him to apologize, and then we argued. But the last time

02:13:12.090 --> 02:13:16.520
I saw him was yesterday morning. It's true. Ads, Miss

02:13:16.570 --> 02:13:22.540
White. Well, we might need to talk to you again at another time. Smith says and turns to go

02:13:22.590 --> 02:13:25.170
back to the courtyard. But then he stops.

02:13:26.110 --> 02:13:29.010
How did you know Mr. Cliff was here, Miss White?

02:13:29.990 --> 02:13:33.270
The secretary blushes. We meet here,

02:13:33.530 --> 02:13:39.560
sometimes, for lunch. Ah, I see. Then the hammering they

02:13:39.610 --> 02:13:41.780
heard in the headmaster's office starts again.

02:13:41.830 --> 02:13:44.410
And the policeman go to the door.

02:13:45.130 --> 02:13:49.430
Detective, says the secretary. You asked about the students.

02:13:50.090 --> 02:13:54.630
They hate Mr. Fletcher. They say he punishes them for every mistake.

02:13:55.570 --> 02:13:58.350
Well, students normally don't like teachers, do they?

02:13:59.130 --> 02:14:04.760
Fletcher is worse, says Cliff. He's a dinosaur. I think

02:14:04.810 --> 02:14:07.850
he hits the boys sometimes, but I can't prove it.

02:14:08.450 --> 02:14:12.790
Smith nods. I'm sure it's nothing. Back in the courtyard,

02:14:13.230 --> 02:14:14.790
Smith stops by the workman,

02:14:15.250 --> 02:14:21.940
who are still sitting and eating their lunch. Listen and observe, West. I suppose you didn't

02:14:21.990 --> 02:14:28.370
notice, but those two are having some sort of romantic affair. Oh, well, yes, I did notice, sir.

02:14:29.450 --> 02:14:33.670
You did? Good. I can teach you a few things,

02:14:33.710 --> 02:14:36.770
I think, West. And Smith nods to the workman.

02:14:37.850 --> 02:14:42.930
Should we speak to the students, sir? West asks as they walk back to the car.

02:14:43.950 --> 02:14:49.510
What for? Well, to see if they know anything. Smith takes the tie from his pocket.

02:14:50.350 --> 02:14:54.030
Their children, West, they can't even put a tie on.

02:14:54.590 --> 02:14:55.950
Let's get back to the city.

02:14:56.890 --> 02:14:59.970
And he throws the tie on the floor. West nods.

02:15:01.130 --> 02:15:04.820
It's best to just nod. But there is something that

02:15:04.870 --> 02:15:11.550
does not make sense. The hammering noise. They heard it in the headmaster's office.

02:15:12.550 --> 02:15:15.450
And they heard it in the corridor with Miss White and Mr Cliff.

02:15:16.430 --> 02:15:23.110
And he looks back at the workman, who were enjoying a slow lunch. But if these men are not hammering.

02:15:23.690 --> 02:15:29.800
Come on, West! Smith shouts. And West nods. Yes, it's

02:15:29.850 --> 02:15:32.800
best to just nod. And somewhere in the dark,

02:15:32.850 --> 02:15:36.230
Mr Fletcher stops hammering his foot against the new wall.

02:15:37.390 --> 02:15:40.010
He is tired now. Very tired.

02:15:41.290 --> 02:15:47.160
He tries to remember exactly what happened. He remembers hitting that idiot of a boy Harris

02:15:47.210 --> 02:15:52.850
because he made a mistake in the geometry class. He remembers another boy telling him to stop,

02:15:53.650 --> 02:15:59.940
then another boy, and another. And then they were all on top of him. At first they only hit him.

02:15:59.990 --> 02:16:04.430
But then he felt school ties being wrapped around his hands and feet and mouth.

02:16:05.390 --> 02:16:08.930
And they lifted him up and carried him to the new wall which was nearly finished.

02:16:09.790 --> 02:16:15.150
And they put him behind it in a deep, dark corner when no one could see him.

02:16:15.890 --> 02:16:19.670
And the light disappeared as they put the last bricks in place.

02:16:20.650 --> 02:16:28.330
He tried desperately to shout. But he could not. And he tried to move. But he could not.

02:16:29.050 --> 02:16:33.910
And for a while he thought that he would die in the deep, dark corner behind the wall.

02:16:34.990 --> 02:16:41.700
But everything will be okay now, he knows. Because after hours of struggling, his foot is now

02:16:41.750 --> 02:16:48.450
finally free to move. And the police will be here soon. He is sure. And they will hear.

02:16:49.370 --> 02:16:52.290
So he continues to hammer his foot against the wall.

02:16:53.510 --> 02:16:56.110
And he hopes that someone hears him soon.

02:17:00.190 --> 02:17:01.370
The Poachers

02:17:04.970 --> 02:17:11.580
My God, just look at that, Junior. Owen says with a smile and he points to the valley and

02:17:11.630 --> 02:17:17.110
green forest below them. Abutru wherean forest, what a beauty,

02:17:17.510 --> 02:17:19.710
Alad. And he looks to his son,

02:17:20.210 --> 02:17:26.460
Owen Junior. And he wants to see a smile on the boy's pale face. But like always,

02:17:26.510 --> 02:17:32.230
there's nothing but disinterest. We saw it last year, Junior says.

02:17:33.150 --> 02:17:38.210
It just looks the same. The same? Well, I suppose,

02:17:38.530 --> 02:17:41.350
but look at its son. Look at how beautiful it is.

02:17:42.030 --> 02:17:48.810
Can there be a finer place in Wales? I don't think so. But Junior only shrugs.

02:17:49.430 --> 02:17:55.920
Owen shakes his head. He remembers when he came here with his father to poach a few rabbits as a

02:17:55.970 --> 02:17:59.180
child. He loved the walk up from the town of

02:17:59.230 --> 02:18:06.960
Sennybridge. He loved the hours of walking and hunting in the forests and valley. But not Junior. Junior

02:18:07.010 --> 02:18:12.710
said that to walk there was stupid because they had a car. And despite all of Owen's arguments,

02:18:13.030 --> 02:18:14.950
the teenager did not change his mind.

02:18:16.010 --> 02:18:21.840
Owen looks back at the car and hopes that no one can see it from the main road. He lifts the two

02:18:21.890 --> 02:18:24.630
gunbags onto his shoulder and starts to walk.

02:18:25.550 --> 02:18:29.010
Come on, son. This is going to be great fun,

02:18:29.830 --> 02:18:33.350
he says. And Junior follows him but says nothing.

02:18:34.470 --> 02:18:41.650
The forest is alive with life. It's the second week of April and spring is certainly here.

02:18:42.390 --> 02:18:48.480
The grass below their feet is green and fresh. The trees are heavy with leaves and the air smells

02:18:48.530 --> 02:18:53.090
so good that Owen forgets about Junior's mood. Smell that,

02:18:53.330 --> 02:18:56.250
son. Good, eh? That's the scent of life,

02:18:56.690 --> 02:19:00.510
the scent of the real whales. Junior lifts his head.

02:19:01.350 --> 02:19:02.470
I can't smell anything.

02:19:03.110 --> 02:19:09.240
What? The flowers, the trees, spring. Oh, that. And the

02:19:09.290 --> 02:19:11.150
boy pulls something out of his pocket

02:19:11.750 --> 02:19:14.650
and Owen sees that it's his MP3 player.

02:19:15.310 --> 02:19:20.290
Whoa, whoa, whoa. He says, wait a minute, son. You don't need that today.

02:19:20.890 --> 02:19:24.810
What? But I want to listen to my music. Music?

02:19:25.550 --> 02:19:29.270
Listen to the forest lad. Listen to the birds in the wind.

02:19:30.110 --> 02:19:36.130
No, you don't need that today. He repeats and he takes the MP3 player from the boy's hands.

02:19:36.810 --> 02:19:40.150
And he tries to avoid the angry look in the boy's dark eyes.

02:19:41.110 --> 02:19:45.810
Dark eyes, just like his mothers. And dark hair, too.

02:19:46.610 --> 02:19:47.590
Just like his mothers.

02:19:48.490 --> 02:19:54.840
Owen is tall and fair, with blue eyes and a healthy complexion from years of work and activity

02:19:54.890 --> 02:20:02.420
outside. Junior is short for his age. Short and thin with a pale and unhealthy complexion from

02:20:02.470 --> 02:20:05.790
hours of playing his computer games and watching television.

02:20:06.890 --> 02:20:13.640
When Owen was a boy of fourteen, he played rugby or football every day. He ran to the

02:20:13.690 --> 02:20:19.410
school in the village just for fun. And he spent every Sunday in the forests with his dad.

02:20:20.210 --> 02:20:26.170
Junior does not like sports. He does not run. And the last time he came to the forest with Owen,

02:20:26.690 --> 02:20:29.370
he said that it was too cold and that the forest was boring.

02:20:30.150 --> 02:20:32.870
But Owen does not like to think about that too much.

02:20:33.690 --> 02:20:36.670
His wife, Rhea, is a good woman and pretty, too.

02:20:37.250 --> 02:20:39.360
But sometimes he cannot help but look at

02:20:39.410 --> 02:20:45.780
Junior and wonder. So he tries not to think about it. Rhea says that it's just his age

02:20:45.830 --> 02:20:51.050
and that all teenage boys prefer computer games and television to walks in the forest.

02:20:51.890 --> 02:20:56.830
And yes, the last time they came to the forest it was a bit cold and they had nothing to do.

02:20:57.510 --> 02:21:05.400
But not this time. No, this time the sun is high and hot. And this time Owen has his old guns

02:21:05.450 --> 02:21:11.480
over his shoulder. And he remembers the way that Junior's expressionless face changed when he

02:21:11.530 --> 02:21:15.390
saw them the night before. These were your grandad's guns,

02:21:15.990 --> 02:21:17.090
he said to him in the kitchen,

02:21:17.710 --> 02:21:19.730
and one day they can be yours if you want.

02:21:20.410 --> 02:21:24.110
Really? And I can shoot things? Animals and things?

02:21:24.750 --> 02:21:30.610
Of course, lad. But I need to show you how first. But I know how. You do?

02:21:31.230 --> 02:21:34.590
Yeah, I saw how in films and on television. Oh,

02:21:34.650 --> 02:21:39.900
in laughed. Oh, well, I need to show you how to do it safely. So we need to go

02:21:39.950 --> 02:21:47.520
up to the old Abattrawaran forest. And Owen knew the boy was interested then. And for the first time in a

02:21:47.570 --> 02:21:53.740
long time he thought that maybe there was something they could do together. Father and son, the way

02:21:53.790 --> 02:21:56.450
it should be. But now, in the forest,

02:21:56.850 --> 02:22:03.100
he's not so sure. The boy looks bored already. And he's not looking at the gun bags with interest

02:22:03.150 --> 02:22:10.600
anymore. Well, maybe when he gets to shoot one. Soon they are deep in the forest. And Owen finds

02:22:10.650 --> 02:22:15.950
a small clearing. He puts the guns next to a tree and takes them out of the bags carefully.

02:22:16.510 --> 02:22:19.650
And yes, there is the interest in the boy's eyes.

02:22:20.470 --> 02:22:23.070
So what can we shoot? Junior asks.

02:22:24.090 --> 02:22:28.050
At this time of year we want to look for rabbit's son. But if we can get a hair,

02:22:28.550 --> 02:22:33.190
even better. What about deer? Can we shoot them? Deer?

02:22:34.110 --> 02:22:39.580
No, son. They're too big. We don't want to carry a deer back to the car. Someone might

02:22:39.630 --> 02:22:43.460
see us. So? And Owen realises that the boy does

02:22:43.510 --> 02:22:49.250
not understand what poaching is. Well, you see, really, we aren't supposed to be here.

02:22:49.890 --> 02:22:57.620
What? Junior says, a scared look in his eyes. Why not? Well, to some people, shooting here is

02:22:57.670 --> 02:23:02.950
illegal. They call it poaching. Which people? Well, the police,

02:23:03.090 --> 02:23:09.180
for example, and the forestry commission. Junior looks around the forest. You mean it's a

02:23:09.230 --> 02:23:16.140
crime? It's illegal. Owen thinks about what to say. Well, it is, son. But only

02:23:16.190 --> 02:23:18.470
like downloading films for free.

02:23:19.170 --> 02:23:22.750
That's a crime. That's illegal. But you still do that,

02:23:22.930 --> 02:23:23.370
don't you?

02:23:23.890 --> 02:23:27.050
Well, this is just like that. No one gets hurt.

02:23:27.830 --> 02:23:34.780
We take one or two rabbits home and we have a little fun. That's okay, isn't it? Junior nods. I suppose

02:23:34.830 --> 02:23:37.170
so. But what if someone sees us?

02:23:37.750 --> 02:23:43.520
Ah, good question, son. Now, not many people come to this side of the forest at this time of the

02:23:43.570 --> 02:23:48.990
year. But if you see someone, you just put the gun down gently in a bush or under some leaves.

02:23:49.990 --> 02:23:55.210
Remember to do it nice and gently and to say hello. If you have no gun, then there's no problem.

02:23:55.950 --> 02:23:58.670
We can come back for the gun another day. Okay?

02:23:59.430 --> 02:24:07.100
So remember, if you see or hear someone,

02:24:07.150 --> 02:24:10.450
Junior looks a bit happier now. And for 20 minutes,

02:24:10.750 --> 02:24:12.930
Owen talks to him about how to hold the gun

02:24:13.530 --> 02:24:19.320
and how to walk with it. While his son listens carefully and asks sensible questions that show

02:24:19.370 --> 02:24:22.970
a real interest. For the first time in many years,

02:24:23.390 --> 02:24:26.270
Owen and Junior seem to be exactly what they are,

02:24:26.750 --> 02:24:32.370
father and son. And Owen notices that the boy actually looks a little bit like him after all.

02:24:33.010 --> 02:24:38.750
And that he also smiles sometimes. Sure, he likes films and television and not sports.

02:24:39.570 --> 02:24:44.490
That maybe this can be their thing. It doesn't have to be every weekend if Junior doesn't want to.

02:24:45.110 --> 02:24:51.460
But occasionally, then maybe in a few years, they can start to go for a pint like Owen and his father

02:24:51.510 --> 02:24:58.820
did. Right then, son, he says, feeling extremely happy and enjoying the excitement in the boy's

02:24:58.870 --> 02:25:04.270
eyes. Now, I'm going to load the guns, then we can follow the path there into the forest.

02:25:05.230 --> 02:25:08.250
Do you remember the rules about the gun, son? Yes,

02:25:08.350 --> 02:25:14.320
dad. Keep the gun pointed down to the ground unless I see a rabbit. Never point the gun at

02:25:14.370 --> 02:25:21.320
you or anyone else. Keep to your left, but not directly behind you. Don't shoot in us. I can clearly

02:25:21.370 --> 02:25:23.050
see that there's no one near the target.

02:25:23.990 --> 02:25:28.390
And don't forget there are two shots and that I should empty the gun before I give it back to you.

02:25:28.970 --> 02:25:35.770
Exactly, says Owen. And he feels full of pride and thinks what a wonderful day this is,

02:25:36.170 --> 02:25:40.870
out in the beauty of the Welsh countryside with his son, and for a while they walk.

02:25:41.730 --> 02:25:47.440
And he's happy about how carefully Junior holds the gun and how he listens and does exactly what

02:25:47.490 --> 02:25:52.210
he should. But then they reach another clearing and they hear something behind the trees.

02:25:52.910 --> 02:25:58.370
Dad, Junior whispers, and he looks scared. What's that noise?

02:25:59.410 --> 02:25:59.990
Is it the police?

02:26:00.970 --> 02:26:06.130
And Owen signals him to be quiet. He tries to see through the trees, but he cannot.

02:26:07.050 --> 02:26:13.710
He thinks there is someone or something moving. And it's big, bigger than a rabbit or a hair,

02:26:14.290 --> 02:26:16.310
but he does not think it can be a person.

02:26:16.850 --> 02:26:19.150
He never sees people up here at this time of year.

02:26:19.810 --> 02:26:24.630
In the summer maybe, but not now. Could it be a park ranger from the Forestry Commission?

02:26:25.470 --> 02:26:30.900
Did someone see the car parked in the trees and called the police? There is a snap from behind

02:26:30.950 --> 02:26:36.760
the trees, and Owen thinks that maybe he should put his gun down in a bush nice and carefully

02:26:36.810 --> 02:26:43.370
like he told Junior to do. Behind him he can hear Junior, who sounds like he is very scared.

02:26:44.180 --> 02:26:48.420
But then Owen sees what is behind the trees. Brown

02:26:48.470 --> 02:26:55.520
hair, big gentle eyes. He laughs and turns back to Junior to tell him to stay calm. But

02:26:55.570 --> 02:27:02.280
before he can do this, Junior, who does not know what is making the noise, remembers his dad's words. If

02:27:02.330 --> 02:27:04.610
you have no gun, then there's no problem.

02:27:05.590 --> 02:27:12.280
But he does not remember the other words. Put it down gently. Owen sees the boy throw the

02:27:12.330 --> 02:27:14.510
gun and tries to shout that it's just a deer,

02:27:15.150 --> 02:27:18.230
but it's too late. There is a loud bang.

02:27:19.090 --> 02:27:24.330
Owen feels an incredible pain in his behind, and he screams and falls to the floor.

02:27:25.050 --> 02:27:30.190
As he lies there, shouting and screaming and trying to pull the pieces of shot from his skin,

02:27:31.070 --> 02:27:35.970
he looks up at the beautiful blue sky and green trees and magnificent forest.

02:27:36.970 --> 02:27:41.590
And he thinks that maybe next time they should just go to the cinema instead.

02:27:42.330 --> 02:27:46.450
Or maybe play a computer game or watch television.

02:27:49.410 --> 02:27:50.270
The card chalk.

02:27:53.730 --> 02:28:00.190
Are you sitting comfortably? Good. I want to tell you a little story that I know.

02:28:00.890 --> 02:28:07.170
Now it's not a very nice story, and I can't promise you that there will be a very happy ending.

02:28:07.950 --> 02:28:12.790
But it's a true story. And that's something that's very important to me.

02:28:13.510 --> 02:28:19.250
And I want you to remember that. Yeah, you need to remember that the truth is important to me.

02:28:19.990 --> 02:28:26.230
And you know what? It's something that's very important to you too. Or it should be.

02:28:27.350 --> 02:28:33.260
So relax a little and listen. See, there was once a young man called Jimmy Lane,

02:28:33.310 --> 02:28:40.010
an Englishman from the great city of London. Well, Jimmy was a nobody in many ways.

02:28:41.330 --> 02:28:43.250
You know what I mean when I say a nobody.

02:28:44.170 --> 02:28:50.840
I mean, Jimmy wasn't rich, and he wasn't too bright, you know? Not very clever, really. And he wasn't

02:28:50.890 --> 02:28:56.190
a funny guy, and he didn't have much luck with the ladies. I know all this to be true.

02:28:56.890 --> 02:29:03.570
I know it because I can read people. And I could see all this when I look to Jimmy Lane. However,

02:29:03.850 --> 02:29:07.710
Jimmy had a talent, a skill, an ability.

02:29:08.630 --> 02:29:15.530
You see, Jimmy could play cards. Poker, Blackjack, Rummy, you name the game, and Jimmy could play it.

02:29:16.310 --> 02:29:22.270
Though he especially liked poker. With a deck of cards in his hands, Jimmy was like a magician.

02:29:23.490 --> 02:29:27.650
He could cut and shuffle the deck like he had eight arms instead of two.

02:29:28.310 --> 02:29:34.720
And he could remember every card he saw in a split second and could use his fingers and little

02:29:34.770 --> 02:29:41.900
tricks to put the cards where he wanted them in the deck. Oh boy, what a talent. What a skill

02:29:41.950 --> 02:29:46.780
Jimmy had. And he had something else too. He had a

02:29:46.830 --> 02:29:50.750
choice, a choice about what to do with this talent.

02:29:51.550 --> 02:29:56.540
You see, there are two things you can do with a talent like that. You can either play the game

02:29:56.590 --> 02:30:03.190
right. You play it straight. This way you enter the competitions. You play your best.

02:30:04.110 --> 02:30:10.650
And you play by the rules. Then slowly you win respect. And slowly you make a name for yourself.

02:30:11.870 --> 02:30:18.570
Or there's the other thing you can do. You can cheat. Do you know what I mean when I say cheat?

02:30:19.470 --> 02:30:22.850
I mean you use the little tricks, the fast fingers,

02:30:23.010 --> 02:30:29.200
and the good memory. But you use it to break the rules of the game. And do you know what choice

02:30:29.250 --> 02:30:33.830
Jimmy Lane made? You got it. He decided to cheat.

02:30:34.730 --> 02:30:40.230
He decided to come to the biggest, richest, most important gambling city in the world.

02:30:41.170 --> 02:30:45.570
And he decided to cheat. That's right. He couldn't wait.

02:30:46.170 --> 02:30:49.360
He wanted everything now. He wanted money.

02:30:49.410 --> 02:30:56.310
He wanted to be famous and he wanted respect. So yeah, he came here to Las Vegas.

02:30:57.210 --> 02:31:05.020
Vegas. Wow. What a city. The lights, the music, the atmosphere. And Jimmy thought he could take it

02:31:05.070 --> 02:31:10.790
all. Huh. What a kid. What a dumb, stupid kid.

02:31:11.830 --> 02:31:14.270
You see, you don't take from Las Vegas.

02:31:15.550 --> 02:31:21.180
Vegas takes from you. But Jimmy, poor, stupid Jimmy, didn't

02:31:21.230 --> 02:31:23.790
know that. So he found a casino,

02:31:24.770 --> 02:31:27.990
a real casino. One of the best in the city.

02:31:29.150 --> 02:31:30.630
And he thought he could do it quickly.

02:31:31.750 --> 02:31:37.830
Play the game, get the money, and run before anyone knew he was there. But he picked the wrong casino.

02:31:38.950 --> 02:31:46.150
He picked my casino. That was a bad decision. But worse than that was his next decision.

02:31:47.150 --> 02:31:52.160
That was when he decided he was good enough and clever enough and lucky enough to play

02:31:52.210 --> 02:31:57.880
at my table. To enter my golden chip competition. A

02:31:57.930 --> 02:32:03.990
competition where only the best of the best get to play. How he made the money to enter,

02:32:04.070 --> 02:32:11.800
I don't know. I think he probably cheated to get that too. But he entered my competition and he used

02:32:11.850 --> 02:32:14.680
his tricks and his fast fingers and he won

02:32:14.730 --> 02:32:20.750
every game until it came to the final. Hank wins,

02:32:20.890 --> 02:32:23.890
stops talking and puts his cigar back in his mouth.

02:32:24.570 --> 02:32:31.540
He looks calm. Happy almost. But there is something very dangerous about Hank win when

02:32:31.590 --> 02:32:37.580
he looks calm and happy. Some people say that the reason his casino is called shark pool

02:32:37.630 --> 02:32:45.440
is because Hank looks a little like a shark. He always wears a gray suit. He always walks in a

02:32:45.490 --> 02:32:50.010
slow manner. And his eyes are always cold and dark.

02:32:50.990 --> 02:32:57.780
But maybe there's another reason for the name of the casino. The truth Jimmy. And Hank throws the

02:32:57.830 --> 02:33:00.050
end of the cigar into the pool in front of him.

02:33:00.970 --> 02:33:03.780
It is night time. And around the roof of the

02:33:03.830 --> 02:33:05.610
30 floor casino and hotel,

02:33:06.430 --> 02:33:13.740
the city of Las Vegas is alive with color. There are people everywhere. But here on the roof there

02:33:13.790 --> 02:33:19.610
are only four people. There is Hank win and his assistants and bodyguards Kenny and Clive.

02:33:20.410 --> 02:33:27.950
Who stand patiently and listen to his speech. And there is Jimmy Lane. Jimmy Lane does not stand.

02:33:28.770 --> 02:33:35.730
Jimmy Lane cannot stand. His hands are tied. His legs are tied. And his mouth is gagged.

02:33:36.510 --> 02:33:44.270
And there is blood on Jimmy Lane. Small cuts on his arms and chest. And he's naked apart from his trousers.

02:33:46.170 --> 02:33:49.490
Now I'm not a cruel man Jimmy. I'm really not.

02:33:49.950 --> 02:33:51.790
I'm a businessman, not a gangster.

02:33:52.490 --> 02:33:58.230
But this city is cruel Jimmy. And sometimes I have to do things to protect my business.

02:33:59.110 --> 02:34:05.200
Do you understand that Jimmy? Do you know how many kids like you come into my casino?

02:34:05.250 --> 02:34:13.180
No? Hundreds. Thousands. They all think they can win. They

02:34:13.230 --> 02:34:15.890
all think they can cheat and beat the casino.

02:34:16.950 --> 02:34:21.970
And Jimmy Lane shakes his head and tries to say something. And his blue eyes are wet.

02:34:22.890 --> 02:34:28.290
Wait a moment Jimmy. Wait one moment. Hank says and he puts his hand on Jimmy's head.

02:34:29.150 --> 02:34:34.130
And Jimmy tries to move away. I don't want you to say anything stupid Jimmy.

02:34:34.980 --> 02:34:39.910
Because this is your last chance. He looks across the pool to his assistant.

02:34:40.990 --> 02:34:44.950
When did they last eat Clive? Last week Mr. When.

02:34:45.710 --> 02:34:48.510
You remember the Mexican? The one stealing from the bar?

02:34:49.350 --> 02:34:57.050
Ah yes. A week. Did you hear that Jimmy? So before I take this gag out of your mouth.

02:34:58.030 --> 02:35:02.510
And before you think about saying something stupid again. Think about this.

02:35:03.350 --> 02:35:06.470
Did you really not think that we were watching you?

02:35:07.270 --> 02:35:08.310
We knew you were cheating.

02:35:09.310 --> 02:35:11.390
We knew you were using your little tricks.

02:35:12.530 --> 02:35:18.170
The only reason you got to the final table to my table Jimmy is because I let you.

02:35:18.930 --> 02:35:21.190
And Hank takes Jimmy's face in his hands.

02:35:22.110 --> 02:35:29.670
The truth Jimmy. I want the truth. It's very important to me. And right now it's very important to you.

02:35:30.330 --> 02:35:35.150
And he takes the gag from Jimmy's mouth. For a moment Jimmy is silent.

02:35:36.050 --> 02:35:37.990
Then he shouts as loud as he can.

02:35:38.830 --> 02:35:41.490
Help me. Somebody we're on the roof. Help me.

02:35:42.190 --> 02:35:45.590
And for a moment Hank is silent. Then he laughs.

02:35:46.510 --> 02:35:47.770
And the two assistants laugh.

02:35:48.810 --> 02:35:52.110
And then the back of Hank's hand slaps Jimmy's face hard.

02:35:53.150 --> 02:35:59.430
Oh Jimmy you really are a stupid kid. We're miles up above one of the loudest cities in the world.

02:35:59.670 --> 02:36:03.570
No one can hear you. No one but me and the boys.

02:36:04.430 --> 02:36:09.250
So Jimmy. One last chance. Tell me. Tell me the truth.

02:36:10.390 --> 02:36:12.810
And Jimmy looks at the pool and the clear water.

02:36:13.710 --> 02:36:14.070
And he nods.

02:36:15.230 --> 02:36:19.510
Okay okay you're right Mr. Win. Everything you say about me is right. I was a nobody.

02:36:19.910 --> 02:36:22.080
I was never rich. I was never too clever. But

02:36:22.130 --> 02:36:23.210
I can play cards.

02:36:23.990 --> 02:36:29.050
I started playing when I was seven or eight with friends at school online in local competitions.

02:36:29.670 --> 02:36:34.530
And I'm good. I mean I'm really good. And yeah that's why I came here. I wanted to win.

02:36:35.110 --> 02:36:38.770
I wanted the respect you talked about. Is that a crime? Is that wrong?

02:36:39.590 --> 02:36:44.470
Hank slaps him again. No Jimmy but cheating in my casino is.

02:36:45.110 --> 02:36:50.390
I didn't cheat. Jimmy shouts and he's angry now. I never cheat.

02:36:50.630 --> 02:36:55.670
You can hit me or you can try to scare me but I never cheat. Hank Win laughs.

02:36:56.370 --> 02:36:59.790
Ha ha. Scare you. You think we want to scare you.

02:37:01.230 --> 02:37:03.890
Clive let them out. He says.

02:37:04.810 --> 02:37:08.750
Clive nods and pushes a button on the control panel next to the pool.

02:37:09.490 --> 02:37:14.630
Below the water a metal door slides open. For a moment there is nothing.

02:37:15.470 --> 02:37:19.190
But then two long gray shapes appear in the clear water.

02:37:20.270 --> 02:37:21.890
And Jimmy starts to shout again.

02:37:22.490 --> 02:37:25.510
You cheated Jimmy. Four aces?

02:37:26.190 --> 02:37:29.790
I know you put those cards in. I don't know how but I know.

02:37:30.490 --> 02:37:34.630
No Mr. Win. I didn't. I promise. I didn't. I don't cheat. I never cheat.

02:37:35.410 --> 02:37:42.210
For a moment Hank Win stops and maybe there's a second where he believes poor Jimmy Lane.

02:37:43.130 --> 02:37:49.180
But then the second is over and he kicks Jimmy in the back and Jimmy falls into the pool with a

02:37:49.230 --> 02:37:57.160
screen. Hank Win does not stop to watch what happens next. He never does. You see he's not a

02:37:57.210 --> 02:38:01.010
cruel man but he sometimes has to do cruel things.

02:38:01.850 --> 02:38:09.540
Come on. He says to Clive and Kenny and they move back to the stairs. But Mr. Win how did you

02:38:09.590 --> 02:38:12.870
know he cheated? We didn't see anything on the cameras.

02:38:13.510 --> 02:38:16.250
Clive asks. Hank shakes his head.

02:38:16.910 --> 02:38:22.710
Idiot. I always take the aces out of the pack and make sure that I get the kings.

02:38:23.270 --> 02:38:26.930
He says and he goes back into the casino to play another game.

02:38:27.770 --> 02:38:34.300
But for a moment Clive and Kenny wait. Stepping backwards so that the waves caused by the feeding

02:38:34.350 --> 02:38:37.660
frenzy do not get at them. For a moment Clive

02:38:37.710 --> 02:38:42.620
is silent. Then he slowly says something as if he

02:38:42.670 --> 02:38:48.970
what he's saying. I changed the pack of cards before the last game.

02:38:49.990 --> 02:38:55.680
I never told Mr. Win. You mean Clive nods. The

02:38:55.730 --> 02:39:02.420
aces were in the pack and for a little while more they wait until the surface of the pool is

02:39:02.470 --> 02:39:06.010
calm again. I guess he was telling the truth then

02:39:06.550 --> 02:39:09.380
and they shut the door on what remains of Jimmy

02:39:09.430 --> 02:39:09.770
Lane.

02:39:12.150 --> 02:39:13.170
The Tube

02:39:15.210 --> 02:39:23.060
The air of the tube platform is hot and dry and the lights weak and unnatural. Sarah considers

02:39:23.110 --> 02:39:28.580
sitting in one of the seats by the wall of the tunnel. But she's so tired that she thinks that

02:39:28.630 --> 02:39:35.480
maybe she'll fall asleep if she rests. So she stands and she looks around at her fellow commuters

02:39:35.530 --> 02:39:41.700
and other passengers. 20 tired faces. Some talking but most

02:39:41.750 --> 02:39:44.050
are silent and waiting like her.

02:39:44.890 --> 02:39:48.570
She looks at the electronic clock. It is 7.36.

02:39:49.770 --> 02:39:52.070
Her train should be here in two minutes.

02:39:53.030 --> 02:39:58.860
For a moment she thinks about how quiet the platform is and how normally she has to fight to get on

02:39:58.910 --> 02:40:04.640
the train in the evening. But there will be no fighting today. The few people that are here

02:40:04.690 --> 02:40:10.850
look half dead, exhausted from a long day at work and made passive by the slow journey home.

02:40:12.190 --> 02:40:19.870
Home. Sarah thinks about her cozy sofa and the Chinese takeaway she will eat while watching TV.

02:40:20.890 --> 02:40:26.060
She can get it as she passes the restaurant, buy a bottle of wine from the local shop and be in

02:40:26.110 --> 02:40:33.250
bed by 10 o'clock. It is a comforting but also rather depressing thought. But that's life.

02:40:33.970 --> 02:40:40.880
Work. Eat. Sleep. She hears the train in the dark tunnel and tells herself to wake up

02:40:40.930 --> 02:40:47.470
and to focus for another 40 minutes. The 40 minutes from Liverpool Street to North Acton.

02:40:48.150 --> 02:40:54.270
The train speeds out of the tunnel and she enjoys the feeling of the wind in her dark brown hair.

02:40:55.210 --> 02:40:58.930
As it stops she sees her reflection in the mirror of the carriage.

02:40:59.670 --> 02:41:02.490
I look like death. She thinks.

02:41:03.530 --> 02:41:08.810
The doors of the carriage is open. A few people get off and Sarah and the commuters get on.

02:41:09.530 --> 02:41:12.410
Inside the carriage it's even hotter than on the platform.

02:41:13.370 --> 02:41:14.770
But it's almost empty too.

02:41:15.530 --> 02:41:21.650
She finds a seat and sits down. Do not fall asleep. She tells herself.

02:41:22.590 --> 02:41:26.540
She looks around the carriage. There are only eight other people there.

02:41:26.590 --> 02:41:31.330
A young couple holding hands and talking quietly on the seat opposite her.

02:41:32.030 --> 02:41:36.770
Two businessmen a few seats to her right. An old lady two seats to her left.

02:41:37.650 --> 02:41:43.410
A mother and son at the far end of the carriage. And a strange looking man in a coat in the corner.

02:41:44.450 --> 02:41:51.910
A strange man. Is he strange? Or are you just being silly? She looks at him.

02:41:52.550 --> 02:41:56.170
She cannot see his face because he has the hood of his sports jacket up.

02:41:56.590 --> 02:42:01.550
But he seems to be asleep. And there's something about his face that she does not like.

02:42:02.350 --> 02:42:08.830
He has a short beard and pale unhealthy skin. And she thinks that his eyes are probably unkind.

02:42:09.990 --> 02:42:12.910
The train starts. And she shakes her head.

02:42:13.790 --> 02:42:17.990
You are an idiot. She tells herself and pulls a book out of her bag.

02:42:18.750 --> 02:42:24.110
On the front there's a picture of a man with a short beard and pale skin and unkind eyes.

02:42:24.930 --> 02:42:26.290
She almost laughs.

02:42:27.270 --> 02:42:30.890
You need to stop reading this rubbish. You're losing your mind.

02:42:31.670 --> 02:42:32.430
She tells herself.

02:42:33.330 --> 02:42:39.230
The man in the corner is probably just a normal guy and not some horrible character from one of her thrillers.

02:42:39.850 --> 02:42:44.450
For ten minutes, she reads her crime novel. She starts to feel better.

02:42:45.410 --> 02:42:50.090
More relaxed and less concerned about the reason why the tube is so quiet today.

02:42:51.070 --> 02:42:52.790
Then the train stops at another station.

02:42:53.570 --> 02:42:56.990
There are loud voices on the platform and Sarah knows why.

02:42:58.130 --> 02:43:01.900
The doors slide open and ten or twelve students from

02:43:01.950 --> 02:43:04.030
Chancery High School enter the carriage.

02:43:05.310 --> 02:43:10.830
The old woman two seats away from her does not look happy and she moves to sit in the seat next to Sarah.

02:43:11.610 --> 02:43:13.090
Do you mind, dear?

02:43:13.870 --> 02:43:16.330
No, of course not, Sarah says and smiles.

02:43:17.410 --> 02:43:20.830
The poor old woman must not feel too comfortable next to the noisy kids.

02:43:21.390 --> 02:43:23.430
But Sarah knows that they're okay.

02:43:24.330 --> 02:43:26.610
She can spot the bad kids from a mile away.

02:43:27.350 --> 02:43:29.090
She is, after all, a teacher.

02:43:30.030 --> 02:43:35.730
No, these kids are okay, but they are loud and it's impossible for Sarah not to hear their conversation.

02:43:37.030 --> 02:43:41.490
And when she hears the topic that they are talking about, she suddenly feels cold.

02:43:42.470 --> 02:43:44.310
Yeah, he killed another one, didn't he?

02:43:44.650 --> 02:43:45.610
Last Friday they reckon?

02:43:46.210 --> 02:43:47.430
The tallest of the kids says,

02:43:48.070 --> 02:43:49.510
I know, sick, isn't it?

02:43:49.890 --> 02:43:51.210
I mean, how many is that now?

02:43:51.830 --> 02:43:52.610
asks another.

02:43:53.570 --> 02:43:54.010
Six?

02:43:54.790 --> 02:43:55.790
No, man, seven.

02:43:56.590 --> 02:43:57.090
Is it?

02:43:57.890 --> 02:44:00.530
Man, you know, the sick thing is that no one knows who it is.

02:44:01.230 --> 02:44:04.230
I don't know if it's a man, a woman, some kid, you get me?

02:44:04.830 --> 02:44:07.170
No, the sick thing is the way he kills them.

02:44:07.750 --> 02:44:12.370
He uses something strange, not a knife, but something long and thin,

02:44:12.590 --> 02:44:15.840
and he stabs them again and again and they just bleed and bleed.

02:44:15.890 --> 02:44:20.730
Yeah, but the weird thing is none of them try to fight, says the tall kid.

02:44:21.490 --> 02:44:22.290
How do you know that?

02:44:22.870 --> 02:44:24.190
Because the newspapers said it.

02:44:24.730 --> 02:44:27.470
They said none of the victims looked like they tried to fight.

02:44:28.390 --> 02:44:32.190
They just, I don't know, let this guy stab them again and again.

02:44:32.970 --> 02:44:34.830
And now there is silence in the carriage.

02:44:35.870 --> 02:44:38.610
And Sarah sees that everyone is listening to the conversation,

02:44:39.670 --> 02:44:41.190
but she does not want to listen.

02:44:41.910 --> 02:44:46.390
And she takes her MP3 player from her bag and puts her headphones in.

02:44:47.150 --> 02:44:52.550
She does not want to think about their conversation or the horrible reason why the tube is so quiet.

02:44:53.570 --> 02:44:56.270
She does not want to think about the stories in the newspapers.

02:44:57.370 --> 02:45:01.610
She does not want to think about what the police are calling the tube murders.

02:45:02.670 --> 02:45:05.190
She closes her eyes and thinks about her sofa.

02:45:06.450 --> 02:45:08.350
Her Chinese takeaway and her bed.

02:45:09.090 --> 02:45:14.930
After a few more minutes, she feels the train stop and she is aware of the kids getting off.

02:45:15.950 --> 02:45:20.290
Just listen to the music, she tells herself, relax and rest.

02:45:21.170 --> 02:45:22.210
But don't sleep.

02:45:23.730 --> 02:45:24.370
Don't?

02:45:25.110 --> 02:45:28.830
But she is exhausted and her head begins to drop.

02:45:29.710 --> 02:45:31.790
And she feels herself falling asleep.

02:45:32.910 --> 02:45:37.150
And she dreams about dark tunnels and strange men watching her.

02:45:37.710 --> 02:45:40.150
Then she feels a sharp pain in her arm.

02:45:40.830 --> 02:45:41.950
And she almost screams.

02:45:42.910 --> 02:45:46.870
She looks to see what the pain is and pulls her headphones from her ears.

02:45:47.730 --> 02:45:50.610
I'm sorry, dear, the old woman says.

02:45:51.530 --> 02:45:55.130
And Sarah sees that the woman's cloth bag is touching her arm.

02:45:55.750 --> 02:45:56.910
Did I wake you up?

02:45:57.650 --> 02:45:59.230
It must be something in my bag.

02:45:59.710 --> 02:46:01.370
I have all my knitting things in here.

02:46:01.910 --> 02:46:04.630
The old woman looks very upset and apologetic.

02:46:05.350 --> 02:46:07.810
It's okay, Sarah says and smiles.

02:46:08.610 --> 02:46:09.530
I was just surprised.

02:46:10.370 --> 02:46:11.910
Oh, you poor thing.

02:46:12.450 --> 02:46:13.130
You look exhausted.

02:46:14.070 --> 02:46:15.750
I'll move my bags so you can sleep.

02:46:16.310 --> 02:46:16.970
It's okay.

02:46:17.310 --> 02:46:18.210
I don't want to sleep.

02:46:18.850 --> 02:46:19.810
No, you relax.

02:46:20.850 --> 02:46:21.910
The old woman says.

02:46:22.730 --> 02:46:25.270
And Sarah thinks the old lady has a kind smile.

02:46:26.130 --> 02:46:31.350
Sarah nods and is about to put her headphones back in her ears when she feels someone watching her.

02:46:32.050 --> 02:46:34.010
And she looks at the corner of the carriage.

02:46:34.630 --> 02:46:35.950
The strange man.

02:46:36.790 --> 02:46:40.050
The man who looks like the evil character from her thriller.

02:46:40.830 --> 02:46:42.130
His eyes are open now.

02:46:42.950 --> 02:46:44.350
And they are watching her.

02:46:45.110 --> 02:46:47.970
She feels a cold shiver move over her body.

02:46:48.790 --> 02:46:51.190
And suddenly she thinks that she feels a little ill.

02:46:51.990 --> 02:46:52.970
Is he watching her?

02:46:53.610 --> 02:46:54.290
She looks again.

02:46:55.270 --> 02:47:00.850
And yes, his hood is still covering his face, but she is sure that his eyes are focused on her.

02:47:01.550 --> 02:47:03.060
She tries to stay calm.

02:47:03.110 --> 02:47:05.790
There are still several people in the carriage.

02:47:06.730 --> 02:47:08.830
The young couple is still there and the two businessmen.

02:47:09.710 --> 02:47:12.890
And the next station is North Acton, her station.

02:47:13.570 --> 02:47:15.450
You just need to get home.

02:47:16.130 --> 02:47:16.950
She tells herself.

02:47:17.890 --> 02:47:20.130
And she feels the train slow down.

02:47:20.870 --> 02:47:22.570
And she knows she must get up.

02:47:23.350 --> 02:47:24.730
Is this your stop, dear?

02:47:25.530 --> 02:47:26.490
The old woman asks.

02:47:27.310 --> 02:47:27.930
Sarah nods.

02:47:28.930 --> 02:47:29.530
Oh, good.

02:47:30.410 --> 02:47:31.210
You're nearly home.

02:47:31.790 --> 02:47:33.790
Nearly time to get some sleep.

02:47:34.930 --> 02:47:36.090
Sarah tries to smile.

02:47:36.890 --> 02:47:38.010
Are you okay, dear?

02:47:38.810 --> 02:47:39.790
You look a little pale.

02:47:40.490 --> 02:47:42.050
Yes, just tired.

02:47:42.910 --> 02:47:44.790
You should put some water on your face.

02:47:45.170 --> 02:47:45.850
You'll feel better.

02:47:46.670 --> 02:47:47.350
Sarah nods.

02:47:48.170 --> 02:47:49.170
I will, thank you.

02:47:49.890 --> 02:47:51.210
I will, she thinks.

02:47:52.110 --> 02:47:54.950
But only if the strange man stays on the train.

02:47:55.790 --> 02:47:58.570
If he follows me, I will shout and scream for help.

02:47:59.370 --> 02:48:02.990
She stands up and looks in the corner of the carriage one more time.

02:48:03.950 --> 02:48:05.610
But it looks like the man is asleep.

02:48:06.630 --> 02:48:08.650
Go, go now, she tells herself.

02:48:09.650 --> 02:48:12.550
The door's open and she quickly walks through them.

02:48:13.250 --> 02:48:17.290
For a few seconds, she walks and then turns back to look at the tube train.

02:48:18.270 --> 02:48:19.050
No one is there.

02:48:20.070 --> 02:48:22.570
No one gets off, apart from her, she is sure.

02:48:23.430 --> 02:48:26.110
The train starts again and she relaxes a little.

02:48:26.710 --> 02:48:28.950
The platform at North Acton is quiet.

02:48:29.790 --> 02:48:31.650
But it always is at this time of the evening.

02:48:32.570 --> 02:48:35.610
She can see the stairs to the street and she wants to walk to them.

02:48:36.210 --> 02:48:37.650
But she feels so weak.

02:48:38.490 --> 02:48:40.490
She decides to go into the station toilets.

02:48:41.730 --> 02:48:43.090
All she needs is a little water.

02:48:43.990 --> 02:48:46.450
She enters the ladies and stands in front of the mirror.

02:48:47.390 --> 02:48:48.170
Her arm hurts.

02:48:49.270 --> 02:48:50.690
So she takes her jacket off.

02:48:51.630 --> 02:48:55.230
And in the mirror, she can see a small drop of blood on her skin.

02:48:56.110 --> 02:48:57.090
It's okay, dear.

02:48:57.410 --> 02:48:58.610
You'll feel better in a moment.

02:48:59.210 --> 02:49:01.350
I gave you a little medicine to help you sleep.

02:49:02.110 --> 02:49:04.070
Sarah jumps at the sound of the voice.

02:49:04.850 --> 02:49:07.530
And in the mirror, she sees the reflection of the old woman.

02:49:08.410 --> 02:49:08.930
What?

02:49:09.770 --> 02:49:12.890
She tries to say, but then she falls to the floor.

02:49:14.030 --> 02:49:17.350
Help me, she says, in a weak voice.

02:49:18.430 --> 02:49:19.710
The old woman smiles.

02:49:21.050 --> 02:49:22.210
But it is a horrible smile.

02:49:23.050 --> 02:49:24.410
I am helping you, dear.

02:49:24.950 --> 02:49:26.030
You poor thing.

02:49:26.810 --> 02:49:29.070
So tired, so exhausted.

02:49:30.030 --> 02:49:31.390
You can't live like that.

02:49:32.130 --> 02:49:33.270
But it's okay now.

02:49:34.050 --> 02:49:35.290
I'm here to help you.

02:49:35.950 --> 02:49:40.350
And the old woman takes a long, thin knitting needle from her bag.

02:49:41.450 --> 02:49:44.090
And Sarah remembers what the kid said on the tube.

02:49:44.870 --> 02:49:46.570
He uses something strange.

02:49:47.730 --> 02:49:50.270
Not a knife, but something long and thin.

02:49:51.090 --> 02:49:53.260
And he stabs them again and again.

02:49:53.310 --> 02:49:56.430
And they just bleed and bleed.

02:49:57.790 --> 02:49:59.010
She tries to scream.

02:50:00.630 --> 02:50:01.890
But it is too late.

02:50:03.950 --> 02:50:06.130
I'll feed her her by audible.
